hadiths[600]{hadithnumber,text}:
"601","Muhammad ibn al-Khattab narrated to us, Muammal narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Husayn, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Amr ibn Hurayth (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would sometimes touch his beard in prayer."
"602","Abu Mamar narrated to us, Abd al-Salam narrated to us, from Yazid al-Dalani, from al-Hasan, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to touch his head and his beard in prayer."
"603","Abu Mamar narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman al-Muharibi narrated to us, from Layth ibn Abi Sulaym, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) with the same, and he did not mention his beard."
"604","Abu Bakr said: al-Fadl ibn Dukayn narrated to us, Abd al-Salam, that is Ibn Harb, narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn Abi Farwah, from Amir ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you feels movement in his stomach, or something, while in prayer, let him place his hand upon his nose and leave."
"605","Al-Harith said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us, Muadh narrated to us, from Qatadah, he said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Do not push away filth of urine or stool during prayer."
"606","Chapter of smiling and thinking in prayer. Ahmad ibn Mani said: Ali ibn Thabit narrated to us, al-Wazi ibn Nafi narrated to us, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Jabir ibn Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: While the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was praying Asr in the expedition of Badr, he smiled in prayer; when he finished the prayer, they said: O Messenger of Allah, you smiled while in prayer? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Mika'il (peace and blessings be upon him) passed by me while returning from pursuing the people, and upon his wing was dust, so he laughed to me, and I smiled to him. I say: Ali is abandoned, Ibn Hibban accused him of fabrication, and al-Wazi is very weak, worthless."
"607","Abu Yala said: Amr ibn Muhammad al-Naqid narrated to us, Ali ibn Thabit narrated to us, the like of it."
"608","Musaddad said: Fudayl ibn Iyad narrated to us, from Mansur, from al-Harith ibn Qays, he said: When you are in a matter of worldly matters, then act alone, and when you are in a matter of matters of the Hereafter, then linger as much as you can, and when you intend good, do not delay it, and when Satan comes to you while in prayer and says: You are showing off, then lengthen it."
"609","Chapter of the prohibition of reciting in bowing and prostration. Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, al-Amash narrated to me, from Malik ibn al-Harith, from Abu Khalid, he said: A man came to Abdullah and said: So-and-so recites while bowing. He said: Men recite the Quran and it does not pass their throats, but when it is firmly rooted in the heart, it takes effect."
"610","Ahmad ibn Mani said: Abu Yusuf narrated to us, al-Hajjaj narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn Hafs, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he forbade a man to recite the Quran while bowing or prostrating."
"611","Chapter of tranquility between the two prostrations. Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Ajlan, Said al-Maqburi narrated to me, he said: I prayed beside Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), and I stood upright upon the front of my feet and my knees, so he struck my thigh until I became tranquil. Sahih mawquf."
"612","Chapter of what one prostrates upon. Abu Dawud said: Abu Utbah, that is Ismail ibn Ayyash, narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Ubaydillah ibn Hamzah ibn Suhayb, he said: I saw Wahb ibn Kaysan prostrating upon the hairline, and I asked him about that, and he said: Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to do it."
"613","Abu Bakr said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz: I said to Wahb ibn Kaysan: O Abu Nuaym, why do you not press your forehead and your nose to the ground? He said: That is because I heard Jabir ibn Abdullah saying: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prostrating upon the upper part of his forehead at the hairline."
"614","Abu Yala said: Muhammad ibn Ismail ibn Abi Saminah narrated to us, Mubashshir ibn Ismail narrated to us, Abu Bakr al-Ghassani narrated to us, from Hakim ibn Umayr, from Jabir ibn Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to prostrate upon his forehead along with the hairline. This chain is weak, and the one before it is likewise."
"615","Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Sumayy, from al-Numan ibn Abi Ayyash, he said: The companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) complained to him about leaning in prostration, so he permitted them to lean upon their knees with their elbows. Mursal."
"616","Yahya narrated to us, from Shubah, from Abd al-Malik ibn Maysarah, from Abu al-Ahwas, he said: Abdullah ibn Masud (may Allah be pleased with him) commanded us when we prostrate to place our elbows and our forearms upon the ground, so I mentioned it to Tawus, and he said: It is false."
"617","Chapter of bowing and prostration and remembrance in them. Ishaq said: Ahmad ibn Ayyub informed us, from Abu Hamzah, from Jabir, that is al-Jufi, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Ibn Masud (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Allah the Exalted does not look at the prayer of a servant who does not raise his lower garment above his heels, and who touches the ground with his palms. Jabir, that is al-Jufi, is abandoned."
"618","Abu Yala said: Dawud narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from al-Ahnaf, that he heard Abu Sallam, he said: Abu Salih al-Ashari informed me that Abu Abdullah al-Ashari (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a man who did not complete his bowing nor his prostration. He said: If this man died upon what he is upon, he would die upon other than the religion of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); as for bowing with prostration, the example of one who prays and does not complete his bowing nor his prostration is like the example of the hungry one who eats nothing but one date or two dates, which avail him nothing. Abu Salih said: I met Abu Abdullah and said: Who narrated this hadith to you? Or did you hear it yourself? He said: He heard it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The commanders of the armies said: Khalid ibn al-Walid, Shurahbil ibn Hasanah, and Amr ibn al-As (may Allah be pleased with them), that they heard it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"619","Ibn Abi Umar said: Waki narrated to us, Ismail ibn Rafi narrated to us, from a man, from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to a man: When you bow, place your hands upon your knees and spread your fingers."
"620","Al-Tayalisi said: Sallam, that is Abu al-Ahwas, narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Sayyar ibn al-Maghrur, he said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) preaching, saying: O people, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) built this mosque, and we were with him, the Muhajirun and the Ansar; when crowding becomes severe, let a man among you prostrate upon the back of his brother. And he saw people praying in the road, and said to them: Pray in the mosque."
"621","Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, and he mentioned it up to his saying: upon the back of his brother."
"622","Musaddad said: Abd al-Wahid narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Abdullah al-Asamm narrated to us, from his uncle Yazid ibn al-Asamm, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he prostrated, the whiteness of his armpits would be seen."
"623","Abu Yala said: Abdullah ibn Umar ibn Aban narrated to us, Abd al-Samad narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from al-Husayn ibn Abdullah, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a rainy day, shielding himself with a cloak with mud upon it when he prostrated."
"624","Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Ajlan, from Ibn Saman, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), that he saw a man prostrating and not bowing, and he said: You have lied; there is no prostration without bowing."
"625","Yahya narrated to us, from Humayd, from al-Hasan, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: We used to supplicate standing and sitting, and glorify in bowing and prostration."
"626","Ahmad ibn Mani said: Yazid narrated to us, Humayd al-Tawil informed us, he said: al-Hasan led us in one of the two afternoon prayers and lengthened, and I saw the trembling of his beard; when he finished I said to him: Were you reciting? He said: Most of it was glorification and supplication. Then he said: Jabir ibn Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with them both) narrated to us, and he mentioned it."
"627","Abu Yala said: Abd al-Ala narrated to us, Mutamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn Uthaym al-Hadrami saying: Uthaym narrated to me, from Uthman ibn Ata al-Khurasani, from his father, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: It was my night with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he slipped away, so I thought he had gone to one of his wives, and I went out jealous, and there he was (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prostrating like a cast-off garment, and I heard him saying: My darkness and my form have prostrated to You, and my heart has believed in You, this is my hand and what I have brought upon myself, O Tremendous One hoped for every tremendous matter, forgive the tremendous sin. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raised his head and said: What brought you out? I said: I had a thought. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Some thought is sin, so ask Allah the Exalted for forgiveness; Jibril (peace and blessings be upon him) came to me and commanded me to say these words that you heard, so say them in your prostration, for whoever says them will not raise his head until he is forgiven."
"628","Abu Bakr said: Muhammad ibn Umar narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Jafar narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Muhammad, from Amir ibn Sad, from his father (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the servant prostrates, he prostrates upon seven bones: his face, his two hands, his two knees, and his two feet; whatever he does not do, he has diminished."
"629","Abd ibn Humayd said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us with this."
"630","I say: Muhammad ibn Umar is al-Waqidi, very weak, except that he was not alone in it, for Abu Yala said: Musa ibn Muhammad ibn Hayyan narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi al-Wazir Abu al-Mutarrif narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Jafar, with the like of it. Abdullah ibn Jafar, the father of Ali ibn al-Madini, alone narrated it, and he is weak, and he erred in its chain; Amir ibn Sad only narrated it from al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib (may Allah be pleased with him). Thus Muslim and the authors of the Sunan brought it."
"631","Chapter of Takbir. Abu Dawud said: Zamah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Jabir ibn Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say takbir when he bowed and when he lowered."
"632","Ibn Abi Umar said: Bishr ibn al-Sari narrated to us, Israil narrated to us, from Thuwayr, from his father, from Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The first who reduced the takbir was al-Walid ibn Uqbah. Abdullah said: They reduced it, may Allah reduce them; I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying takbir whenever he bowed, whenever he prostrated, and whenever he raised his head."
"633","Chapter of a slight action not invalidating prayer. Musaddad said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from one who narrated to him, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that she saw no harm in a man moving his ring to his fingers, guarding himself with it in prayer."
"634","Abu Yala said: Abu Hisham al-Rifai narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Muawiyah ibn Yahya al-Sadafi, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was praying in my house, and Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) came and stood beside him on his right; a scorpion came toward the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and when it drew near him, it was turned away from him, and it came toward Ali, so he took the sandal and killed it while praying. When he finished his prayer, he (may Allah be pleased with him) said: May Allah kill it; it came toward the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then it was turned away from him, then it came intending me. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw no harm in killing it in prayer."
"635","Abu Bakr said: Bakr ibn Isa narrated to us, Muhammad, that is Ibn Abi Layla, narrated to us, from Atiyyah, from Abu Said (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: al-Husayn (may Allah be pleased with him) came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was prostrating, and rode upon his back; he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took him by his hand and stood while he was upon his back, then he bowed, then he released him, and he (may Allah be pleased with him) went."
"636","Chapter of raising the hands. Abu Bakr said: Waki narrated to us, from Hammad, from Bishr ibn Harb, that he heard Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) saying: Your raising your hands in prayer is an innovation; by Allah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did no more than this, meaning with his finger."
"637","Al-Harith said: Abu al-Nadr narrated to us, from Sulayman, from Humayd, one who heard the Bedouin narrated to me, saying: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praying; he raised his head from bowing and raised his palms until they were level with or reached the lobes of his ears, as if they were two fans."
"638","Chapter of Tashahhud. Abu Dawud said: Zuhayr narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, he said: I met al-Aswad and said to him: Abu al-Ahwas adds, from Abdullah, in the tashahhud: al-Mubarakat. He said: Go to him and forbid him this, and tell him: Abdullah taught Alqamah the tashahhud and counted them upon his hand. Sahih."
"639","Musaddad said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq, from Muharib ibn Dithar, he said: I heard Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to teach us the tashahhud as the teacher teaches children."
"640","Abd al-Wahid narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman narrated to us with it, and he added: upon the minbar."
"641","Yahya narrated to us, from Zayd al-Ammi, from Abu al-Siddiq, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) used to teach us the tashahhud upon the minbar, as boys are taught in the school."
"642","Abu Bakr said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq, from Abu Burdah, from Abu Musa (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I have been given the openings of speech, its conciseness, and its endings. We said: Teach us of what Allah the Exalted taught you. So he taught us the tashahhud."
"643","Muhammad ibn Abi Umar said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Mamar informed us, from Khusayf, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a dream, and I said: O Messenger of Allah, there is disagreement upon us about the tashahhud; so-and-so said such, and so-and-so said such, and Ibn Masud (may Allah be pleased with him) said such. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Excellent is the tashahhud of Ibn Masud. Al-Tirmidhi brought it abridged."
"644","Abu Yala said: Abu Mamar Ismail ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Abd al-Salam ibn Harb narrated to us, from Budayl ibn Maysarah, from Abu al-Jawza, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not add in the two rakahs beyond the tashahhud."
"645","Chapter of supplication in the tashahhud. Abu Yala said: Shabab narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Humran narrated to us, Safwan narrated to us, from Asim ibn Kulayb, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: I entered the mosque while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in prayer, placing his right hand upon his right thigh, pointing with the forefinger, saying: O Turner of hearts, make my heart firm upon Your religion."
"646","Chapter of Taslim. Al-Harith said: Muhammad ibn Umar narrated to us, Yaqub ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Sasah narrated to us, from Ayyub ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Sasah, from Abbad ibn Tamim, from Abdullah ibn Zayd (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The opening of prayer is purification, its prohibition is takbir, and its release is taslim."
"647","Muhammad ibn Umar narrated to us, Said ibn Muslim ibn Banak narrated to us, from Abu Malik al-Jazari, from Ata ibn Yasar, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave one taslim to his right."
"648","Muhammad ibn Umar narrated to us, Dawud ibn Khalid, Ibn Abi Sabrah, Sulayman ibn Bilal, and Ali ibn Umar ibn Ata, all of them, narrated to us, from Umar ibn Ata, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), that he gave one salam facing the qibla."
"649","Muhammad ibn Umar narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, that he heard al-Zuhri saying: I saw Qabisah ibn Dhuayb, when he gave salam, giving one salam facing the qibla. Al-Zuhri said: I mentioned that to Abdullah ibn Mawhab. He said: I asked Qabisah about that, and he said: I saw Zayd ibn Thabit giving one salam facing the qibla."
"650","Muhammad ibn Umar narrated to us, Said ibn Ata ibn Abi Marwan al-Aslami narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: I prayed behind Umar, behind Ali, and behind Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with them), and I saw each of them giving salam to his right and to his left."
"651","Muhammad ibn Umar narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Hibban, from Abu Muadh al-Juhani, from Uqbah ibn Amir (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) giving salam to his right and to his left: Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah, peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah."
"652","Abu Bakr said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Razin, from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), that he gave salam to his right and to his left, then he stood."
"653","Waki narrated to us, from Hurayth, from al-Shabi, from al-Bara (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to give salam to his right and to his left: Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah, until the whiteness of his cheek was seen."
"654","Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Shubah, Abu Ishaq narrated to me, from Harithah ibn Mudarrib, he said: Ammar (may Allah be pleased with him) was our commander for a year, and he never led us in a prayer except that he gave salam to his right and to his left: Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah, peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah."
"655","Chapter of speech after prayer. Abu Bakr said: Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, al-Amash narrated to us, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Silah ibn Zufar: I heard Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) saying after prayer: O Allah, You are Peace and from You is peace, blessed are You, O Possessor of Majesty and Honor. Then I prayed beside Abdullah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with them both) and heard him saying them, so I said to him: I heard Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) saying like what you say. Abdullah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with them both) said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say them at the end of his prayer."
"656","Musaddad said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, al-Ala ibn al-Musayyab narrated to us, from Amr ibn Murrah, he said: A man prayed beside Abdullah ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), and when he finished his prayer he sat supplicating, and he mentioned it. He said: Then he prayed beside Abdullah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with them both), and he said the like of it. The man said to him: This is a supplication I heard from your brother Abdullah ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both)."
"657","Al-Tayalisi said: Qays narrated to us, from Aidh ibn Nusayb, from Jabir ibn Samurah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pointing with his finger in prayer, and when he gave salam I heard him saying: O Allah, I ask You all good, what I know of it and what I do not know, and I seek refuge in You from all evil, what I know of it and what I do not know."
"658","Abd ibn Humayd said: Ali ibn Asim narrated to us, from Abu Harun, from Abu Said al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave salam from prayer, he said: Glory be to your Lord, the Lord of Might, above what they describe, to the end of the verse."
"659","Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah said: Hushaym narrated to us, Abu Harun al-Abdi narrated to us, from Abu Said al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) more than once saying at the end of his prayer when leaving, and he mentioned it."
"660","Abd said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Abu Harun, from Abu Said (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say after every prayer, I do not know whether after the taslim or before the taslim, and he mentioned it."
"661","Al-Harith said: Abu al-Nadr narrated to us, Sufyan, or al-Ashjai, narrated to us, from Sufyan with it."
"662","Abu Yala said: Ishaq, that is Ibn Abi Israil, narrated to us, Hammad, that is Ibn Zayd, narrated to us, from Abu Harun, and he mentioned it like the hadith of Ali ibn Asim. Abu Harun alone narrated it, and he is weak."
"663","Abu Bakr said: Ibn Fudayl narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Yahya ibn Jadah, from a man who narrated to him, from Umm Malik al-Ansariyyah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: She brought a skin of ghee to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the hadith. He said: Then he taught her to say after every prayer ten times: Glory be to Allah ten times, and praise be to Allah ten times, and Allah is Most Great ten times."
"664","Abu Yala said: Amr ibn Hafs narrated to us, Said ibn Rashid narrated to us, from al-Hasan ibn Dhakwan, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Bara (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever seeks Allah the Exalted's forgiveness after every prayer three times, saying: I seek forgiveness from Allah, besides Whom there is no god, the Living, the Sustainer, and I repent to Him, his sins will be forgiven, even if he fled from battle."
"665","Abu Yala said: Shayban narrated to us, Uqbah ibn Abdullah al-Rifai al-Asamm narrated to us, from al-Jad Abu Uthman, from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) prayed in the mosque of Banu Rifah here, and he commanded a man among his companions to call the adhan, and he led them in Subh; when he finished his prayer, he turned to the people and said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed with his companions, he turned to the people and said: O Allah, I seek refuge in You from a deed that disgraces me, O Allah, I seek refuge in You from wealth that makes me transgress, O Allah, I seek refuge in You from a companion who destroys me, O Allah, I seek refuge in You from hope that distracts me, O Allah, I seek refuge in You from poverty that makes me forget."
"666","Al-Bazzar said: Talut ibn Abbad narrated to us, Bakr ibn Khunays narrated to us, from Abu Imran al-Jawni, from al-Jad, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him). He said: We know none who narrated it from Anas except al-Jad, nor from him except Abu Imran, and none narrated it except Bakr, and he is not strong; thus he said, but Uqbah followed him as you see."
"667","Abu Yala said: Sufyan ibn Waki narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Humayd, from Abu al-Malih, Abd allah ibn Rabah narrated to us, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) used to say after the two rakahs of Fajr: O Allah, Lord of Jibril, the hadith. Al-Nasai brought it unrestricted."
"668","Abd said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: The poor Muslims complained of what the rich were favored with over them, and said: O Messenger of Allah, these our brothers believed as we believed, prayed as we prayed, and fasted as we fasted, but they have excellence over us in wealth: they give charity and join kinship, while we are poor and do not find that. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Shall I not inform you of something which, if you do it, you will attain their excellence? Say after every prayer: Allah is Most Great eleven times, and praise be to Allah eleven times, and glory be to Allah eleven times, and there is no god but Allah alone, without partner, eleven times; you will attain the like of their excellence. That reached the rich, and they said the like of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded them. They came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, our brothers say the like of what we say. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: That is the bounty of Allah, He gives it to whom He wills. Shall I not give you glad tidings, O company of the poor? The poor believers will enter Paradise before their rich by half a day, five hundred years."
"669","Al-Bazzar said: al-Walid ibn Amr ibn al-Sakan narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Zibriqan narrated to us, Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated to us, and he mentioned it. He added: And Musa recited: And indeed a day with your Lord is as a thousand years of what you count. He said: We know it only from this route, and its defect is Musa ibn Ubaydah. Ibn Majah brought some of it in Zuhd."
"670","Abu Yala said: Abd al-Ala narrated to us, Bishr ibn Mansur narrated to us, Umar ibn Nabhan narrated to us, from Abu Rashid, from Jabir ibn Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Three things, whoever comes with them with faith will enter through whichever of the gates of Paradise he wills, and will be married from the houris as he wills: whoever pardons his killer, and pays a hidden debt, and recites after every prescribed prayer: Say, He is Allah, One. Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Or one of them, O Messenger of Allah? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Or one of them. The rest of remembrance after prayer will come, Allah willing, in the chapter of remembrance."
"671","Chapter of the merit of remembrance after Subh prayer until the sun rises; a hadith will come, Allah willing, in the Duha prayer. Musaddad said: Hafs ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Juhadah, from al-Hakam ibn Utaybah, from a man of Banu Darim, he said: al-Hasan ibn Ali (may Allah be pleased with them both) married a woman from us and dwelt among us; a man of the quarter made food and invited the quarter and invited al-Hasan (may Allah be pleased with him). He said: I did not see al-Hasan (may Allah be pleased with him) answer him. He said: I saw al-Hasan (may Allah be pleased with him) gesturing to a freedman of his. When al-Hasan (may Allah be pleased with him) rose and left, I came to ask his freedman what delayed him from the invitation and what he was gesturing to him. He said: I met al-Hasan (may Allah be pleased with him), greeted him, and he returned to me and greeted me, and said: What brought you, O so-and-so? Do you have a need? I said: O son of the Messenger of Allah, I came to ask your freedman what delayed you from the invitation and what you were gesturing to him. Al-Hasan (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I will tell you that: As for what delayed me from it, I was fasting, and as for what I was gesturing to him, I was asking him: Has the sun risen or not? Then al-Hasan (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever prays Subh then sits remembering Allah the Exalted until the sun rises, it will be for him a covering or a veil from the Fire."
"672","Abu Bakr said: Musab ibn al-Miqdam narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abu Hazim, that he sat beside Iyas ibn Sahl al-Ansari of Banu Saidah in their mosque, and he said: Turn to me. I greeted him, and he said: O Abu Hazim, shall I not narrate to you from my father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: That I pray Subh then sit in my place remembering Allah the Exalted until the sun rises is dearer to me than charging upon excellent horses in the cause of Allah the Exalted, from when I pray Subh until it rises."
"673","Chapter of leaving from prayer. Abu Dawud said: Qays, that is Ibn al-Rabi, narrated to us, from Umayr ibn Abdullah, from Abd al-Malik ibn al-Mughirah al-Taifi, from Aws al-Thaqafi (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: We came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I saw him turning from his right and from his left."
"674","Abu Yala said: Zuhayr narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Yahya ibn Said informed us, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Hibban: He informed him that his uncle Wasi ibn Hibban was standing praying in the mosque, while Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) was leaning his back against the qibla of the mosque. When Wasi finished, he left from his left toward Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) and sat with him. Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) said to him: What prevents you from leaving from your right? He said: Because I saw you, so I left toward you. Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) said: You have done well. People say: When you are praying and you finish, then leave from your right. Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) said: When you are praying and you finish, then leave if you wish from your right, and if you wish from your left."
"675","Chapter of the prostration of recitation in prayer and otherwise, and the permissibility of bowing at the prostration of recitation. Ishaq said: al-Nadr ibn Shumayl informed us, Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, he said: I heard al-Aswad narrating from Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) that he used to say about a surah whose end is a prostration: Recite and prostrate, then stand and recite and bow; and if you wish, bow in al-Araf, al-Najm, Iqra, and the like. This chain is sahih mawquf."
"676","Musaddad said: Hammad informed us, from Ayyub, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from two men, both better than Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: One of them prostrated in When the sky is split, or in Read in the name of your Lord, and the other did not prostrate, and the one who prostrated was better than the one who did not prostrate; if he was not Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), then he is better than Umar (may Allah be pleased with him). The hadith of Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) about When the sky is split already passed in recitation in prayer."
"677","Yahya informed us, from Malik, from al-Zuhri, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) recited al-Najm, then prostrated, then stood and recited another surah."
"678","Abdullah narrated to us, from Abu al-Awwam al-Bahili, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The prostration is only upon one who sits for it."
"679","Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah said: Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, Isa ibn al-Mukhtar narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from a man called Humayd Abu Abdullah, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prostrated in When the sky is split ten times."
"680","Abu Yala said: Abu Bakr narrated to us, and he mentioned it."
"681","Musaddad said: Abd al-Warith narrated to us, from Ayyub, from al-Hasan and Abu Qilabah, they said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: There is no prostration in al-Mufassal."
"682","Hammad narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, the like of it."
"683","Al-Harith said: Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, Yunus narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn Khuzaymah, from his uncle, he said: Khuzaymah saw in what the sleeper sees that he prostrated upon the forehead of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) lay down for him and said: Fulfill your dream. So he prostrated upon his forehead."
"684","Abu Yala said: al-Jarrah ibn Makhlad narrated to us, al-Yaman ibn Nasr, the flour merchant, narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Sad narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir narrated to me, Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf narrated to me, he said: I heard Abu Said (may Allah be pleased with him) saying: I saw in what the sleeper sees as if I were under a tree, and as if the tree were reciting Sad; when it came to the prostration, it prostrated and said in its prostration: O Allah, forgive me a sin by it, O Allah, remove from me a burden by it, and bring about for me gratitude by it, and accept it from me as You accepted the prostration of Your servant Dawud. I went in the morning to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him. He said: Did you prostrate, O Abu Said? I said: No. He said: You are more entitled to prostration than the tree. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited Surah Sad, then came to the prostration, and said in his prostration what the tree said in its prostration."
"685","Chapter of the prayer of the excused. Ahmad ibn Mani said: Qurran ibn Tammam narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Amir al-Aslami, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever can prostrate, let him prostrate, and whoever cannot, let him not raise anything to his face; his prostration shall be bowing, and his bowing shall be gesturing with his head. In its chain are two weak narrators."
"686","Abu Yala said: Abu al-Rabi narrated to us, Hafs ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Ata, from Jabir ibn Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) visited a sick person, and I was with him, and saw him praying and prostrating upon a pillow, so he forbade him and said: If you can prostrate upon the ground, then prostrate, otherwise gesture, and make the prostration lower than the bowing. Hafs is weak."
"687","Abu Yala said: Muhammad ibn Bakkar narrated to us, Hafs ibn Umar narrated to us, Mukhtar ibn Fulful narrated to us, from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed upon the ground in the obligatory prayer sitting, and in glorification he sat upon the ground and gestured."
"688","Chapter of the Istikharah prayer. Abu Yala said: Zuhayr narrated to us, Yaqub ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, Isa ibn Abdullah ibn Malik narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Ata, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Said al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: When one of you intends a matter, let him say: O Allah, I seek good from You by Your knowledge, and I seek power from You by Your power, and I ask You of Your tremendous bounty, for You decree and I do not decree, and You know and I do not know, and You are the Knower of the unseen. O Allah, if such-and-such, in the matter you intend, is good for me in my religion, my livelihood, and the outcome of my affair, otherwise turn it away from me and turn me away from it, then decree good for me wherever it is; there is no power nor strength except by Allah, the High, the Tremendous."
"689","Ibn Hibban said: Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Madini narrated to us, Yaqub narrated to us with it."
"690","Chapter of urging two prostrations after every prayer. Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Shubah, from Abu Hamzah, Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) said: If you can, do not pray a prayer without prostrating two prostrations after it, then do so. This chain is sahih. It is as if what is meant by the two prostrations is two rakahs, and by prayer is the obligatory; and it is possible that he held prostration for forgetfulness even if he did not forget, as a precaution in case he forgot, and Allah knows best. A report from Abdullah ibn Shaqiq the Tabii supporting that will come, Allah willing, in the book of forgetfulness."
"691","Chapter of what one whom something befalls in his prayer should do. Abu Bakr said: Malik ibn Ismail narrated to us, Jafar al-Ahmar narrated to us, from Abu Harun, from Abu Said (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Glorification is for men, and clapping is for women. Abu Harun is weak."
"692","Ubaydah ibn Humayd narrated to us, Ibn Abi Layla narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him), the like of it."
"693","Abu Yala said: Ishaq, that is Ibn Abi Israil, narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Ayyub, from Ubaydullah ibn Zahr, from Ali ibn Yazid, from al-Qasim, from Abu Umamah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I used to seek permission from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); if he was in prayer, he glorified, and if he was not in prayer, he gave me permission."
"694","Chapter of the merit of walking to mosques at night. Abu Bakr said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir, from Makhul, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Whoever walks in the darkness of night to the mosque will meet Allah the Exalted with light on the Day of Resurrection. Its men are trustworthy, except that it is broken between Makhul and the Companion (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"695","Al-Harith said: Dawud narrated to us, Maysarah narrated to us, from Abu Aishah, from Yazid, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah and Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them), they said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us and mentioned a long hadith in it: Whoever walks to a mosque among the mosques, for every step he takes he has ten good deeds, ten evil deeds are erased from him, and ten degrees are raised by it. This hadith is fabricated."
"696","Abu Yala said: Salih ibn Malik narrated to us, Abd al-Ala ibn Abi al-Musawir narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Ata, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No Muslim performs ablution well, then walks to a house among the houses of Allah to pray in it a prescribed prayer, except that Allah, Mighty and Majestic, writes for him a good deed for every step."
"697","Abu Bakr and Abd both said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, al-Dahhak ibn Nibras informed us, from Thabit al-Bunani, from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: Prayer was established, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out to the mosque walking, with me shortening steps, and he said: I only did this so that the number of our steps in seeking prayer increases."
"698","Abu Yala said: Abu Bakr narrated to us with this. I say: al-Dahhak is weak in memory, and what is preserved about this is mawquf upon Zayd ibn Thabit (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"699","Al-Harith said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Said narrated to us, from Aban, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I went out intending the mosque, and there was Zayd ibn Thabit (may Allah be pleased with him); he placed his hand upon my shoulder, leaning upon me. I kept dragging my steps in youthfulness. Zayd (may Allah be pleased with him) said to me: Shorten your step, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever walks to the mosque, for every step he has ten good deeds. Aban is weak."
"700","Musaddad said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, from Yahya al-Ghassani, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Your walking to the mosque and your return to your family are equal in reward. The hadith of Abu Umamah and Thawban (may Allah be pleased with them both): My Lord came to me in the best form, the hadith, already passed at length in the chapter of perfecting ablution, in it is walking upon feet to congregations, and in it is awaiting prayer."
"701","Ibn Abi Shaybah said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Hajari, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No man performs ablution, then comes to a mosque among the mosques, taking a step, except that Allah, Mighty and Majestic, writes a good deed by it, removes a sin by it, and raises him a degree by it."
"702","Book of supererogatory prayers. Chapter of completing the obligatory from the voluntary already passed in the chapter of the greatness of prayer. Chapter of unrestricted supererogatory prayers. Abu Yala said: Zakariya al-Wasiti narrated to us, Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, Zurarah ibn Abi al-Khallal al-Ataki narrated to us, he said: I heard Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever prays twelve rakahs in a day, Allah the Exalted will forbid his flesh to the Fire. He said: I have not left them since."
"703","Ishaq ibn Abi Israil narrated to us, Abd al-Wahid al-Haddad narrated to us, Sadaqah ibn Abi Sahl narrated to us, from Abu al-Mualla al-Tufawi, Yusuf ibn Abdullah narrated to me, he said: I came to Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him) in his illness in which he died, and he said: O son of my brother, what brought you to this land, and what concerned you with it? I said: Nothing concerned me except the bond between you and my father. Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Evil is this hour of lying. I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever performs ablution then stands and prays two rakahs or four rakahs, obligatory or non-obligatory, perfecting bowing and prostration in them, then seeks Allah's forgiveness, except that he will be forgiven."
"704","Musaddad said: Khalid narrated to us, Amr ibn Yahya narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Abi Ziyad, from the servant of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), a man or a woman, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: Do you have a need? Until one day he said: O Messenger of Allah, my need. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: And what is your need? He said: My need is that you intercede for me on the Day of Resurrection. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: And who guided you to this? He said: My Lord. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Then help me with abundance of prostration."
"705","Abu Yala said: Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, Yahya ibn Salih narrated to us, from Jabir ibn Ghanim al-Sulafi, from Ibn Suhayb, from his father Suhayb (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A man's voluntary prayer where people do not see him equals his prayer before people's eyes twenty-five times."
"706","Al-Abbas ibn al-Walid narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, that he heard Ata ibn Yasar narrating from Maymunah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah, Blessed and Exalted, said: Whoever harms a friend of Mine has deserved My war; My servant does not draw near to Me with the like of performing My obligations, and he continues to draw near to Me with supererogatory acts until I love him; when I love him, I am his leg with which he walks, his hand with which he strikes, his tongue with which he speaks, and his heart with which he understands; if he asks Me, I give him, and if he calls upon Me, I answer him; and I have not hesitated about anything I do as I hesitate about his death, for he dislikes it, and I dislike displeasing him. This is weak. I say: In the chapter from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) with al-Bukhari, and from Aishah and Anas (may Allah be pleased with them both)."
"707","Chapter of the middle prayer. Musaddad said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, he said: Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) held that it is Subh, meaning the middle prayer."
"708","Chapter of Tahajjud. Ishaq said: Isa ibn Yunus informed us, Wasil ibn al-Saib narrated to us, from Abu Sawrah, from Abu Ayyub (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed Tahajjud, he prostrated between every two rakahs."
"709","Abd ibn Humayd said: Muhammad ibn Ubayd narrated to us, Wasil narrated to us with his chain and wording: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to use the siwak at night two or three times; when he stood at night, he prayed four rakahs and did not speak anything, nor command anything, and he gave salam after every two rakahs. This chain is weak."
"710","Abu Bakr said: Musab ibn al-Miqdam narrated to us, from Zaidah, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, the nephew of Hudhayfah narrated to me, from Hudhayfah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) one night to pray with his prayer; he opened the prayer and recited a recitation neither light nor loud, a good recitation, chanting in it, making us hear. Then he bowed about a surah's length, then raised his head and said: Allah hears whoever praises Him, Possessor of power, dominion, pride, and greatness. Then his standing was about a surah's length; he prostrated about that until he finished al-Tuwal, with darkness of night upon him. Abd al-Malik said: It is the voluntary of night."
"711","Ahmad ibn Mani said: Jarir narrated to us, from Mansur, from Mujahid, he said: Ja'dah and I entered upon a man of the Ansar who had companionship. He said: They mentioned before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a freedwoman of Banu Abd al-Muttalib, and said: She stands the night and fasts the day. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: But I sleep and pray, and fast and break fast, the hadith."
"712","Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Jadah, he said: It was mentioned to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned it."
"713","Abu Yala said: Abu Hammam al-Walid ibn Shuja narrated to us, my father narrated to me, that Ziyad ibn Khaythamah narrated to him, from Abu Yahya, seller of fodder, from Mujahid, from Muadh ibn Jabal (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned night standing, and his eyes overflowed until his tears flowed down, and he said: Their sides forsake their beds."
"714","Al-Hasan ibn Hammad narrated to us, Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from Hisham, from his father, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divided Surah al-Baqarah in two rakahs."
"715","Ahmad ibn Mani and Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah both said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Amr informed us, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Uthman, that is al-Taymi, he said: I saw Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) at the Maqam one night, having advanced with recitation of the Quran in one rakah, then he left. Its chain is hasan. The hadith of Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) about night prayer in the expedition of Hudaybiyah is from the campaigns."
"716","Al-Tayalisi said: Sharik narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray witr at the call to prayer."
"717","Abu Bakr and Musaddad said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, the like of it, except that he said: the first call."
"718","Ahmad ibn Mani said: Yahya ibn Ishaq narrated to us, Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, from al-Harith ibn Yazid, from Ziyad ibn Nuaym, from Muslim ibn Mikhraq, he said: I said to Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her): With us are people who recite the Quran two and three times in a night. She said: Those are people who recited and did not recite. I saw myself standing with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the complete night, and he did not pass a verse of hope except that he asked his Lord and supplicated, nor a verse of fear except that he supplicated his Lord and sought refuge."
"719","Abu Yala said: Kamil ibn Talhah narrated to us, Ibn Lahiah narrated to us with it, he said: He stands the complete night with recitation of al-Baqarah, Al Imran, al-Nisa, and the rest like it."
"720","Al-Harith said: Umar ibn Said narrated to us, Said narrated to us, from Makhul, from Muhammad ibn Suwayd al-Fihri, from Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I met the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after the night prayer, and said: O Messenger of Allah, permit me to worship with your worship, and he mentioned the hadith. He said: He faced the qibla and placed me at his right, then recited the Opening of the Book, then began Surah al-Baqarah, not passing a verse of mercy except that he asked, nor of fear except that he sought refuge, nor of a parable except that he reflected, until he completed it. Then he said takbir, and I heard him saying in his bowing: Glory be to my Lord, the Tremendous, until I thought he says: and with His praise. He remained in his bowing near his standing, then raised his head, said takbir, and prostrated, and I heard him saying in his prostration: Glory be to my Lord, the Most High. I think he says: and with His praise. He remained in his prostration near his bowing, then rose until he finished his two prostrations, then recited the Opening of the Book, then began Al Imran likewise, then I heard the call to Fajr. Hudhayfah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I never worshipped a worship harder upon me than it."
"721","Al-Harith said: Abu al-Nadr narrated to us, Sufyan, or al-Ashjai, narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Ali ibn al-Aqmar, from Abu Muslim, from Abu Said al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: When a man prays at night and wakes his family and they pray two rakahs, they are written among the men who remember Allah much and the women who remember."
"722","Abu Yala said: Harun ibn Maruf narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Makhramah ibn Bukayr narrated to me, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: I mentioned standing, and some of them said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Its half, its quarter, the interval of milking a she-camel."
"723","Abdullah ibn Umar ibn Aban narrated to us, Anbasah ibn Abd al-Wahid narrated to us, from Ayyub ibn Utbah, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Qilabah, from al-Numan ibn Bashir (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: When one of you sleeps with the intention to pray at night, let him make a handful of dust beside him; when he wakes, let him take a handful with his right hand, then throw to his left."
"724","Salih Abu Mamar narrated to us, Sallam ibn Abi Khubzah narrated to us, from Yunus ibn Ubayd, from al-Hasan, from Samurah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us to pray at night, little or much, and to make that witr."
"725","Al-Hasan ibn Hammad narrated to us, Abu Yahya al-Kufi narrated to us, from Abu Said al-Shami, from Makhul, from Wathilah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Counting verses is in the voluntary, not the obligatory."
"726","Abd ibn Humayd said: Ya'la ibn Ubayd narrated to us, Abu Bakr al-Madani narrated from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would use the tooth-stick at night two or three times; whenever he slept and woke, he used the siwak, performed ablution, and prayed two rak'as, or rak'as."
"727","Musaddad said: Khalid narrated to us, from Mutarrif, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade that a man raise his voice in recitation before Isha and after it, confusing his companions in prayer."
"728","Abu Bakr said: Malik ibn Ismail narrated to us, Khalid ibn Abdullah narrated, with this."
"729","Abu Ya'la said: Wahb ibn Baqiyya narrated to us, Khalid narrated with it, except that he said: confusing his companions, while the people were praying."
"730","Abdullah ibn Awn al-Kharraz narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Bishr narrated, from Mis'ar ibn Kidam, from Qatada, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood until his feet or his leg swelled, so it was said to him: Has Allah not forgiven you what preceded of your sin and what followed? So he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Shall I not be a grateful servant."
"731","Al-Bazzar said: al-Hasan ibn Muhammad al-Umawi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Bishr narrated, Mis'ar narrated with it. I say: It is defective, and the well-known is from Ziyad ibn Ilaqa, from al-Mughira ibn Shu'ba (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"732","Abu Ya'la said: al-Hasan ibn al-Sabbah narrated to us, Mu'ammal narrated, Sulayman ibn al-Mughira narrated, from Thabit, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) felt something, and in the morning it was said: O Messenger of Allah, the trace of pain is indeed visible upon you! He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Despite what you see, I recited last night the seven long surahs."
"733","Abu Dawud said: Muhammad ibn Abi Humayd narrated to us, from Awn ibn Abdullah, from his father, from Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raised his gaze to the sky, then lowered it, so we said: O Messenger of Allah, why did you do this? He said: I wondered at two angels from the angels who descended to the earth seeking a servant in his prayer place but did not find him, then they ascended to their Lord and said: Our Lord, we used to write for Your believing servant in his day and night such-and-such deeds, but we found that You had confined him in Your snare, so we wrote nothing for him; so He, Blessed and Exalted, said: Write for My servant his deed in his day and night, and diminish nothing of it for him; upon Me is his reward; I confined him, so for him is the reward of what he used to do."
"734","Ibn Abi Umar said: Marwan narrated to us, from Iyas, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), in marfu' form: Whoever among you becomes drowsy in the mosque, let him move to his bed until he understands what he says."
"735","Chapter: Standing in Ramadan. Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Ibrahim ibn Maysara, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) met the people at the standing in prayer, and said: What remains of the night is better than what has passed of it."
"736","Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba said: Yazid narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Uthman told us, from al-Hakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray in Ramadan twenty rak'as and the witr."
"737","Abd ibn Humayd said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Abu Shayba - that is Ibrahim - narrated, with this."
"738","Chapter: The command to perform voluntary prayers in houses. Abu Ya'la said: Musa ibn Muhammad ibn Hayyan narrated to us, Abu Bakr al-Hanafi told us, Abdullah ibn Nafi' narrated, al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated, he said: I heard al-Hasan ibn Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with them both) say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Pray in your houses, and do not take them as graves, and do not take my house as a festival, and send prayers and peace upon me, for your prayer and your peace reach me wherever you are."
"739","Uthman - that is Ibn Abi Shayba - narrated to us, Abu Khalid - that is al-Ahmar - narrated, Ziyad narrated, from Mu'awiya ibn Qurra, three men who asked Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) about prayer in the mosque told me, and he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The obligatory prayer is in the mosque, and the voluntary in the house. I say: Ibn Maja brought it through another route from Umar (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"740","Chapter: Voluntary prayer upon the mount. Musaddad said: Attaf ibn Khalid narrated to us, Umar ibn Abdullah ibn Urwa ibn al-Zubayr told me, he said: I heard Abdullah say: I came with al-Zuhri to Sham from the Yarmuk expedition, and I used to see him pray upon his mount wherever it faced."
"741","Chapter: Dislike of raising the voice with the Quran. Al-Harith said: Aswad ibn Amir - Shadhan - narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salama narrated, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Anas, he said: Ziyad came to Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) and said to him: Recite, so he recited and raised his voice, so Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) lifted the cloth from his face upward and said: Is this what you do?! Hammad said: One who witnessed al-Hasan told me, he said: A man raised his voice near al-Hasan, so he lifted a handful of pebbles and struck his face, and said: What is this?!"
"742","Al-Harith said: Muhammad ibn Bakkar narrated to us, Anbasa ibn Abd al-Wahid narrated, Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated, from Abu al-Nadr, from Jabir ibn Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us on a night in Ramadan - while the people were praying - and said: Let not some of you raise the voice over others, for that harms the one praying. And the hadith of al-Harith from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) was mentioned recently."
"743","Chapter: Prohibition of strain and hardship in worship. Al-Harith said: Ishaq narrated to us, Sharik narrated, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Umm Salama (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: The most beloved of deeds to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was what the servant persists in, even if little."
"744","Sa'id ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Wuhayb narrated, Ishaq ibn Suwayd al-Adawi narrated, from a Kufan man called Jabala, that a youth devoted himself at the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so his father went to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: My son has exhausted himself in worship, so he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Order him to be gentle with himself."
"745","Sa'id ibn Yunus Abu Yunus narrated to us, Hammad narrated, from al-Jurayri, from Abdullah ibn Shaqiq, from Mihjan ibn al-Adra' (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: It reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that a man in the mosque was prolonging the prayer, so he came to him, then took him by his shoulder, then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah has approved ease for this nation, and disliked hardship for it - he said it three times - and this one has taken hardship and left ease; then he pulled him away, and he was not seen after that."
"746","Abu Ya'la said: Abu al-Khattab narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abd al-Malik narrated, Isra'il narrated, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Burda, from Abu Musa (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The wife of Uthman ibn Maz'un (may Allah be pleased with them both) entered upon the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and they saw her of poor appearance, so they said to her: There is no man in Quraysh richer than your husband! She said: We get nothing from him; as for his day, he is fasting, and as for his night, he is standing. He said: So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered, and they mentioned that to him. He said: So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) met him and said: O Uthman, have you no example in me? He said: What is that, O Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? May my father and mother be ransom for you. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: As for you, you fast the day and stand the night, yet your family has a right upon you, and your body has a right upon you; so pray and sleep, and break fast and fast. He said: So the woman came to them after that perfumed as if a bride, and they said to her: What?! She said: We have got what the people got."
"747","Musaddad said: Muhammad ibn Abi Adi narrated, from Ash'ath, from al-Hasan, that Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: When one of you rises at night and the Quran becomes confused for him, let him sleep. I say: Its chain is broken, and its origin is in the two Sahihs."
"748","Chapter: Voluntary prayer in travel. Abu Ya'la said: al-Azraq ibn Ali narrated to us, Hassan ibn Ibrahim narrated, Yusuf narrated, from Abu Ishaq, from Haritha ibn Mudarrib, from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) woke at Badr the next morning, he had kept that entire night alive, while he was traveling."
"749","Chapter: The regular prayers and guarding them. Musaddad said: Bishr narrated to us, Abu Harun al-Ghanawi narrated, from Abu Sulayman, Abu Yahya told me, he said: Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) said: O Abu Yahya, did you not see that I slept tonight missing the witr?! Ibn Makhrama and another with him came to me, and they distracted me from the witr, so I slept until morning, and the maid woke me; so I said to her: See, has the sun risen? She said: No; so I prayed the two rak'as of Fajr, then I said: See, has the sun risen? She said: No; so I prayed the Fajr prayer."
"750","Al-Harith said: Ya'la narrated to us, a shaykh called Abd al-Hakam narrated, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Upon you are the two rak'as of Fajr, for in them are precious rewards."
"751","Ibn Abi Umar said: Waki' narrated to us, from Hisham, from Ibn Sirin, from A'isha (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited quietly in the two rak'as before Fajr, and he used to recite in them: Say, O disbelievers, and: Say, He is Allah, One."
"752","Waki' narrated to us, Yazid ibn Ibrahim narrated, from Ibn Sirin, from A'isha (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would stand in them about as long as one recites the Fatiha of the Book."
"753","Abu Ya'la: Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mutawakkil - Abu Ayyub al-Basri - narrated to us, Yahya ibn Wadih Abu Tumayla narrated, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated, from Abdullah ibn Ma'bad, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite in the two rak'as of Fajr, in the first rak'a: The Messenger has believed, until he completed it, and in the second, from Al Imran: Say, O People of the Book, come to a word equal between us and you, the verse. I say: Muslim brought it through another route, he said: In the first: Say, We believe in Allah and what was sent down to us, which is in al-Baqara, and the rest similar."
"754","Ishaq said: Sulayman ibn Harb told us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated, from Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud, from Abu Salih, from Umm Habiba, with the hadith: Whoever prays twelve rak'as in the day... the hadith; Asim said: The companions of Abdullah sought them with the obligatory prayers. I say: Its origin is in the Sunan, without this saying of Asim."
"755","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Asbat ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Bashir Abu Ismail narrated, from an Ansari man, from his father (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever prays four before Zuhr, it is like freeing a slave from the children of Isma'il."
"756","Ibn Abi Umar said: Waki' narrated to us, Bashir ibn Salman - that is Abu Ismail - narrated, from al-Qasim ibn Safwan al-Ansari, from his father (may Allah be pleased with him), the same."
"757","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Husayn ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Shu'ba narrated, from Humayd, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: They were not as keen in guarding anything voluntary as they were upon prayer before Zuhr."
"758","Abu Bakr said: Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, Isa ibn al-Mukhtar narrated, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Yazid ibn al-Bara, from his father (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he used to pray four before Zuhr."
"759","Abu Ya'la said: Abu Kurayb narrated to us, Bakr narrated, with this."
"760","Abu Bakr said: Ibn Abi Ghanniya narrated to us, al-Salt ibn Bahram narrated, from some of his companions, from Hudhayfa ibn Usayd (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I saw Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him), when the sun declined, pray four long ones, so I asked him, and he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pray them, so I asked him, and he said: The gates of heaven are opened when the sun declines, and are not closed until Zuhr is prayed, so I love that a deed of mine be raised to Allah, Mighty and Majestic."
"761","Abu Ya'la said: Harun ibn Ma'ruf narrated to us, Yahya ibn Sulaym narrated, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn Sa'id the mu'adhdhin narrate from Abdullah ibn Anbasa, he said: I heard Umm Habiba bint Abi Sufyan (may Allah be pleased with them both) say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever guards four rak'as before Asr, Allah builds for him a house in Paradise. I say: Abu Dawud narrated it through another route from Umm Habiba (may Allah be pleased with her) with the wording: before Zuhr."
"762","Musaddad: Yazid narrated to us, Shu'ba narrated, from Abu al-Tayyah, from Abdullah ibn Abi al-Hudhayl, he said: I invited a man from the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to my house, and when the Maghrib caller called, he prayed; so I asked about that, and he said: Ubayy ibn Ka'b (may Allah be pleased with him) used to pray them."
"763","And with it to Shu'ba, from Sulayman ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Rashid ibn Yasar, he said: I bear witness upon five of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from those who pledged under the tree, that they used to pray two rak'as before Maghrib."
"764","Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, Mansur told me, from his father, he said: Abu Bakr, Umar, and Uthman did not pray the two rak'as before Maghrib."
"765","Yahya narrated to us, from Shu'ba, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, from his father, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf (may Allah be pleased with him) used to pray in his house after Maghrib two rak'as."
"766","Al-Mu'tamir narrated to us, he said: I heard my father narrate from a man, from Ubayd, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he was asked: Did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) command prayer after the prescribed prayer? He said: Between Maghrib and Isha. Shu'ba followed him, from al-Taymi, he said: We were in the gathering of Abu Uthman, and a man appeared to us, and narrated to us from Ubayd, with it."
"767","Al-Harith said: al-Hasan ibn Qutayba narrated to us, Abu al-Hasan al-Massisi narrated, Abu Ali - who had raided the Romans with us, and was a righteous worshipping man - narrated, from Abu Khaythama, from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), in marfu' form to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever prays two rak'as after the two rak'as of Maghrib, reciting in each rak'a the Fatiha of the Book and Say, He is Allah, One, fifteen times, will come on the Day of Resurrection and it will be said to him: This is among the truthful, so he passes them; then it will be said: This is among the martyrs, so he passes them; then it will be said: This is among the prophets, so he passes them; then it will be said: This is among the angels, so he passes them, and he is not veiled until he reaches the Throne of the Most Merciful. I say: This is a fabricated text."
"768","Musaddad said: Yahya ibn Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: It used to be said: The witr is upon the people of the Quran; so I said: What do you command your daughter with? He said: I command her with two rak'as after Isha, and she was a daughter of five or six years."
"769","Abu Ya'la said: Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Adhrami narrated to us, Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated, from Isra'il, from Maysara ibn Habib, from al-Minhal ibn Amr, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, from Hudhayfa (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was praying between Maghrib and Isha, and he kept praying until he prayed Isha."
"770","Chapter: The Witr. Al-Tayalisi said: Shu'ba narrated to us, from Abu al-Tayyah, from a man of Banu Anaza, from a man of Banu Asad, he said: Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) came out to us when the caller announced the prayer, and said: Your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded the witr, and set for it this hour; call the adhan, O Ibn al-Nabbah."
"771","Abd ibn Humayd said: Abdullah ibn Maslama narrated to us, Khalid ibn Ilyas narrated, from Ibrahim ibn Ubayd ibn Rifa'a, he said: I entered upon Jabir ibn Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with them both) in Mecca, and found him praying sitting, and he mentioned the hadith; he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: No man prays Isha in congregation, then prays after it what occurs to him, then prays witr before he sleeps, except that night is as if he met Laylat al-Qadr."
"772","Ibn Abi Umar said: Waki' narrated to us, Khalid ibn Abi Karima narrated, from Mu'awiya ibn Qurra, he said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, morning came upon me and I did not pray witr. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The witr is only at night - he said that three times - then he said the third time: Go and pray witr."
"773","Musaddad said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) said: The shrewd are those who pray witr at the beginning of the night, and the strong are those who pray witr at the end of the night."
"774","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Muharrar narrated, from Qatada, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: I was commanded with the witr and the sacrifice, but they were not made binding. Its chain is weak."
"775","Musaddad said: Sallam narrated to us, Umm Shubayb told me, from her sister Umm Abdullah, she said: She saw A'isha (may Allah be pleased with her) praying behind the Maqam, and she prayed witr with one rak'a, reciting in it Surat Ibrahim."
"776","And with it: That she heard A'isha (may Allah be pleased with her) say: When you hear the cry, pray witr with one rak'a."
"777","Al-Harith said: Khalid ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, Abbad ibn al-Awwam narrated, Hajjaj narrated, from Qatada, from Zurara ibn Abi Awfa, from Imran ibn Husayn (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray witr with three, reciting in the first with Glorify, in the second with Say, O disbelievers, and in the third with Say, He is Allah, One. Al-Nasa'i brought it through another route, limiting to Glorify."
"778","Abu Ya'la said: Sa'id ibn al-Ash'ath narrated to us, Abd al-Malik ibn al-Walid ibn Ma'dan told me, Asim narrated, from Zirr, from Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite in the witr, in the first rak'a: Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High, in the second: Say, O disbelievers, and in the third: Say, He is Allah, One."
"779","Al-Harith said: al-Nadr narrated to us, Shu'ba narrated, from al-Hakam: I said to Miqsam: I pray witr with three, then the call is made, then I go out; so he said: It is not proper except with five or seven. Al-Hakam said: So I told Mujahid and Yahya ibn al-Jazzar about it, and they said to me: Ask him, from whom is this? He said: From the trustworthy, A'isha and Maymuna (may Allah be pleased with them both)."
"780","Abu Bakr said: Ghundar narrated to us, from Shu'ba, with it, and he did not mention his referring to Mujahid and Yahya."
"781","Ishaq said: Al-Nadr ibn Shumayl, Shu'ba narrated, from al-Hakam, he said: I said to Miqsam: I pray witr with three, then I go out to prayer; he said: There is no witr except with seven or five. So I met Mujahid and Yahya ibn al-Jazzar and mentioned to them; they said: Ask him, from whom? He said: From the trustworthy, from the trustworthy, from A'isha and Maymuna, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"782","He said: And Muhammad ibn Ja'far told us, Shu'ba narrated, with this chain, the same."
"783","Musaddad said: Yahya narrated, from Shu'ba, Abu Na'ama told me, from Abu Tamima, he said: When Abu Musa (may Allah be pleased with him) led us in the morning prayer, he would recite to us; then Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) came, and a man beside me asked him about the witr, so he said: Three is more beloved to me than one, five more beloved to me than three, and seven more beloved to me than five."
"784","Isa narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn al-Fadl narrated, from Ibrahim, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed witr upon his donkey, while facing Khaybar."
"785","Abu Bakr said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, from Aban ibn Abi Ayyash, from Ibrahim, from Alqama, from Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I spent the night with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to see how he performs qunut in his witr, so he performed qunut before bowing; then I sent my mother, Umm Abd (may Allah be pleased with her), and said to her: Spend the night with his wives (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and see how he performs qunut in his witr; so she came to me and told me that he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed qunut before bowing. Aban is abandoned."
"786","Chapter: The Duha prayer. Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Abu Ahmad al-Zubayri narrated to us, Hanzala narrated, from Abd al-Karim, he said: Al-Hasan and al-Husayn (may Allah be pleased with them both) told us that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray Duha, and said: Whoever prays it, a house is built for him in Paradise, and forgiven for him are the sins in the hours of the day."
"787","Abu Ya'la said: Abu al-Ash'ath narrated to us, Salama ibn Raja narrated, Sha'tha told us, she said: I saw Abdullah ibn Abi Awfa (may Allah be pleased with him) pray two rak'as, so his wife said to him: You prayed only two rak'as! He said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pray Duha two rak'as when given glad tidings of the conquest and the head of Abu Jahl. I say: Part of it is in the Sunan of Ibn Maja."
"788","Ishaq: al-Nadr ibn Shumayl narrated to us, Abu Qurra al-Asadi told us, I heard Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib narrate from Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: There is no man who comes to an open space of land and prays there Duha two rak'as, then says: O Allah, to You is praise; I have become Your servant upon Your covenant and promise; You created me and I was nothing; I seek Your forgiveness for my sin, for my sins have burdened me and surrounded me, except that You forgive them for me, so forgive them, O Most Merciful of the merciful, except that Allah forgives him in that sitting his sin, even if like the foam of the sea."
"789","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Abu Mu'awiya narrated to us, Sa'd ibn Tarif narrated, from Umayr ibn Ma'mum, from al-Hasan ibn Ali (may Allah be pleased with them both), in marfu' form: Whoever prays Fajr, then sits until the sun rises, then prays two rak'as, Allah forbids him for the Fire to taste him or touch him."
"790","Ishaq: al-Nadr ibn Shumayl told us, Hammad - that is Ibn Salama - narrated, Ma'bad told us, a man in the Damascus mosque told me, from Awf ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him) sat with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), or the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat with him, and said: O Abu Dharr, have you prayed Duha? He said: No. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Stand and pray Duha. He said: So he prayed, then he came and mentioned the hadith."
"791","Al-Harith said: Yunus ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Hammad narrated, from Ma'bad ibn Hilal al-Anbari, a man in the Damascus mosque told me, from Awf ibn Malik, similar."
"792","Abu Ya'la said: Hudba narrated to us, Hammad narrated, with it."
"793","Abu Bakr said: Ibn Numayr narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Hakim ibn Hakim, from Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Hudhayfa ibn al-Yaman (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out to the lava field of Banu Mu'awiya, so I followed his trace until he appeared upon it, and he prayed Duha eight rak'as, prolonging in them; then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Hudhayfa, did I keep you long? I said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I asked Allah, Mighty and Majestic, in it for three things; He gave me two and withheld one from me: I asked Him not to let another over my nation prevail over it, so He gave it to me; I asked Him not to destroy it with years of famine, so He gave it to me; and I asked Him not to make its violence among themselves, so He withheld it from me."
"794","Al-Harith said: Ali ibn al-Ja'd narrated to us, Shu'ba told us, from al-Hakam, from a man who told him, from Umm Salama (may Allah be pleased with her): That she used to pray Duha eight rak'as sitting, so it was said to her: A'isha (may Allah be pleased with her) prays it four! She said: A'isha (may Allah be pleased with her) is a young woman, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The prayer of one sitting is half of the prayer of one standing."
"795","Abu Bakr said: Hatim ibn Ismail narrated to us, from Humayd ibn Sakhr, from al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayra (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent an expedition, and they gained great spoils and returned quickly; so a man said: O Messenger of Allah, we have not seen an expedition quicker in return nor greater in spoils than this expedition; so he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Shall I not tell you of one quicker in return and greater in spoils than it: a man who performs ablution in his house and perfects it, then heads to the mosque and prays in it the morning prayer, then follows it with the Duha prayer; he has returned quicker and gained greater spoils."
"796","Abu Ya'la narrated it in his Musnad, from Abu Bakr."
"797","And Ibn Hibban, from Abu Ya'la. And al-Bazzar brought it from the hadith of Abu Hurayra (may Allah be pleased with him) also; he said - that is Abu Hurayra -: So Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: O Messenger of Allah..."
"798","Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Ajlan, from al-Qa'qa' ibn Hakim, from his grandmother Rumaytha, she said: I saw A'isha (may Allah be pleased with her) pray Duha eight rak'as, so I said to her: Something you saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do, or something he commanded you with?! She said: I will not tell you anything from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about them, but if my father were raised for me from the grave on condition that I leave them, I would not leave them."
"799","Yahya narrated to us: from Mis'ar, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, from al-Qasim, he said: A'isha (may Allah be pleased with her) used to pray Duha and prolong it."
"800","Yahya narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, Abu Salama told me, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: None guards the Duha except an oft-returning one."
"801","Ibn Abi Umar said: A shaykh from the people of Madina, whom we visited at the top of the pass, narrated to us, he said: From Umar adh-Dhakwani, from one who told him, from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him), that he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came while I was nine or ten years old, and said: O Anas, increase purification, your life will be increased, and pray Duha, for it is the prayer of the oft-returning... the hadith, and the rest of it comes, Allah willing, in the Book of Admonitions."
"802","Abu Ya'la said: Harun ibn Ma'ruf narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated, Umar ibn Muhammad told me, from Abu Rafi', that he told him, from Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As (may Allah be pleased with them both): That he said to Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him): O uncle, grant me good. He said: Yes, nephew. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: O Abu Dharr, if you pray Duha two rak'as, you are not written among the heedless; if you pray it four, you are written among the doers of good; if you pray it six, no sin follows you; if you pray it ten... and if you pray it twelve, a house is built for you in Paradise thereby."
"803","Abu Ya'la said: Shayban narrated to us, Tayyib ibn Sulayman narrated, he said: I heard Amra say: I heard A'isha (may Allah be pleased with her) say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever prays Fajr then sits in his place, not engaging in idle talk about any worldly matter, and remembers Allah until he prays Duha four rak'as, exits his sins as on the day his mother bore him, with no sin upon him."
"804","Hudba narrated to us, al-Muharibi narrated, al-Ahwas ibn Hakim narrated, from Abdullah ibn Ghabir, from Utba ibn Abd as-Sulami, from Abu Umama (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever prays the dawn prayer in congregation, then remains in his sitting place until he prays the Duha supererogatory prayer, for him is the reward of a complete Hajj and Umra, his Hajj and his Umra complete."
"805","Abd ibn Humayd said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Ibn Uyayna narrated, from Ayyub, from al-Qasim, from Ibn Abi Awfa (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The prayer of the oft-returning is when the young camels' feet burn. I say: This is a sound chain, except that it is defective; the preserved in this is from al-Qasim ibn Awf, from Zayd ibn Arqam, as Muslim brought it from the hadith of Ayyub, and from the hadith of Qatada also, from al-Qasim."
"806","Abd said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated, Husam ibn al-Misakk narrated, from Qatada, from al-Qasim ibn Awf, Zayd ibn Arqam (may Allah be pleased with him) told us, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to them in the mosque of Quba and saw them praying Duha, so he said: This is the prayer of the oft-returning. He said: So they used to pray it when the young camels' feet burn. I say: And this differs from the wording of Muslim, for his wording is: That Zayd ibn Arqam (may Allah be pleased with him) saw people praying Duha, and said: They knew that prayer at other than this hour is better; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The prayer of the oft-returning is when the young camels' feet burn."
"807","Al-Harith said: Muhammad ibn Umar narrated to us, Umar ibn Ishaq narrated, that he heard Umar ibn al-Hakam say: I heard Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) say: I never saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pray Duha at all. Umar ibn al-Hakam said: So I mentioned this to Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas (may Allah be pleased with him), and he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to leave a deed fearing the people would see it, so he would do it privately; and I pray it. Sa'd (may Allah be pleased with him) says that. I say: Muhammad ibn Umar is al-Waqidi, and others differed from him in this from Abu Sa'id (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"808","Abd said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Fudayl ibn Marzuq narrated, Atiyya told me, Abu Sa'id (may Allah be pleased with him) told me, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray Duha until it was not thought he left it, and leave it until it was not thought he prays it."
"809","Ishaq said: Sufyan told us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Abdullah ibn al-Harith, he said: Ibn Abbas said, coming upon this verse: Glorifying evening and morning, he said: This is the ishraq prayer, meaning eight rak'as at the beginning of the day."
"810","Chapter: The ruling of one who abandons prayer. Ishaq said: Abd ar-Razzaq told us, Muhammad ibn Rashid narrated, from Makhul, from a man, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever abandons prayer deliberately, the protection of Allah is free of him."
"811","Abd ar-Razzaq said: And a shaykh from the people of Sham told us, from Makhul, he said: Whoever the protection of Allah is free of, has disbelieved."
"812","Baqiyya ibn al-Walid told us, Iyad ibn Abi Humayd narrated, they said: I heard Makhul say about one who says: Prayer is from Allah but I do not pray it, and zakat is from Allah but I do not pay it, he said: He is asked to repent; if he repents, else he is killed."
"813","Al-Mawsili told us, from Hammad ibn Zayd, from Ayyub: About one who says: Prayer is from Allah, Mighty and Majestic, but I do not pray it: his neck is struck from here - and Ishaq pointed to his nape - there is no disagreement among the imams about it."
"814","Chapter: Forgetfulness. Musaddad said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Humayd ibn Tarkhan, he said: Abdullah ibn Shaqiq led us in Asr prayer and made with us two prostrations, though we saw no mistake; when he saluted, they mentioned that to him; they said: We saw no mistake! He said: I conversed with myself."
"815","Ibn Abi Umar said: Al-Muqri narrated to us, Haywa narrated, Yazid ibn Abi Habib told me, Abd ar-Rahman ibn Shimasa told me, he said: Amr ibn al-As (may Allah be pleased with him) prayed with the people, and stood from his tashahhud, so the people shouted at him; they said: Glory be to Allah, Glory be to Allah; so he prayed as he was; when he completed his prayer he made two prostrations, then said: O people, what you intended was not hidden from me, and nothing prevented me from sitting except what I did of the Sunna."
"816","Al-Harith said: Yunus ibn Muhammad narrated to us, al-Layth narrated, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, he said: Abd ar-Rahman ibn Shimasa told him that Uqba ibn Amir (may Allah be pleased with him) stood in his prayer, and he mentioned the same."
"817","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Abu Mu'awiya narrated to us, Ismail ibn Abi Khalid narrated, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, from Sa'd (may Allah be pleased with him): That he rose in the two rak'as, so they glorified for him, so he completed standing, then made the two prostrations of forgetfulness until he departed; he said: You thought I would sit; I did as I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do."
"818","Abu Ya'la said: Amr ibn Muhammad an-Naqid narrated to us, Abu Mu'awiya narrated; Amr said: I did not hear anyone raise it except Abu Mu'awiya."
"819","He said: And Waki' narrated to us, from Ismail, and he mentioned it as mawquf. And al-Mughira ibn Shubayl narrated it, from Qays, from al-Mughira."
"820","Al-Harith said: Asim ibn Ali narrated to us, Ikrima ibn Ammar narrated, from Damdam ibn Jaws, he said: I entered upon Abu Hurayra and Abdullah ibn Hanzala (may Allah be pleased with them both), while they were sitting in the mosque when the sun declined; so Abdullah ibn Hanzala (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) led us in Maghrib prayer, and did not recite in the first rak'a, forgetting; when he stood in the second rak'a he recited the Mother of the Quran and a sura, then returned and recited the Mother of the Quran and a sura, then continued until he completed his prayer, then made the two prostrations of forgetfulness."
"821","Affan narrated to us, Hammad - meaning Ibn Salama - narrated: Thabit told us, from Sila (may Allah have mercy on him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever prays a prayer mentioning in it nothing of worldly matter, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, gives him whatever He is asked therein."
"822","Abu Ya'la said: Amr ibn Muhammad narrated to us, al-Ala ibn Hilal narrated, Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysa, from Abu al-Fayd, from Mu'awiya as-Sulami, he said: Mu'awiya ibn Abi Sufyan (may Allah be pleased with them both) led us in Maghrib three, and stood after two rak'as, so they glorified for him, so he gestured to them; when he completed his prayer and saluted, he departed and addressed them; he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do as you saw me do; were it not that I saw him do it, I would not do it."
"823","Abu Muammar Ismail ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Hakim ibn Nafi' narrated, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, from A'isha (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The two prostrations of forgetfulness suffice for every addition and decrease."
"824","Abu Kurayb narrated to us, Hafs ibn Bishr al-Asadi narrated, Hakim ibn Nafi' narrated, and he mentioned it. Hakim is weak."
"825","Chapters of Jumu'a. Chapter: The virtue of Jumu'a and the hour in which answering of supplication is hoped. Abu Bakr said: Waki' narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Yazid ar-Riqashi, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Jibril (peace be upon him) came to me with a white mirror in which was a black spot; so I said: What is this? He said: This is Jumu'a, in it is an hour."
"826","Said ibn Yahya al-Himyari narrated to us, ad-Dahhak ibn Humra narrated, from Yazid ibn Khumayr, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The days were shown to me, and shown to me among them was Friday; it was like a beautiful mirror, and in its middle was a black spot; so I said: What is this black? He said: This is the hour."
"827","Abd ar-Rahman ibn Muhammad al-Muharibi narrated to us, from Layth, from Uthman, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Jibril (peace be upon him) came to me with Jumu'a, and it was like a white mirror in which was like a black spot; so I said: O Jibril, what is this? He said: This is Jumu'a. I said: And what is Jumu'a? He said: For you therein is good. I said: And what is for us therein? He said: It will be a festival for you and for your people after you, and the Jews and Christians will be followers of you. I said: And what is for us therein? He said: For you therein is an hour no Muslim servant agrees with, asking Allah therein anything of the matter of this world and the Hereafter, except Allah gives it to him - if he has a share therein - else He stores for him with Him what is better than it - if he has no share - or he seeks refuge with Him from an evil written upon him, except He averts from him of calamity what is greater than it. I said: And why is that?! He said: Because your Lord, Blessed and Exalted, took in Paradise a valley of white musk; so when it is the Day of Resurrection He descends from Illiyyin upon His Kursi, then the Kursi is surrounded by pulpits of gold crowned with jewels, then the prophets are brought and sit upon them, then the pulpits are surrounded by chairs of light, then the martyrs are brought until they sit upon them, and the people of the chambers descend and sit upon the sand hill; then their Lord manifests to them, then says: Ask Me, I give you; so they ask Him pleasure; so He says: My pleasure, I settle you in My abode and grant you My honor; so ask Me, I give you; so they ask Him pleasure; so He witnesses to them that He is pleased with them. It was said: So opened for them is what no eye has seen, no ear has heard, and never occurred to a human heart. He said: And that is the extent of your departure from Jumu'a. He said: Then the prophets, the truthful, and the martyrs ascend with Him, and the people of the chambers return to their chambers, and it is a white pearl, or a red pearl, or a green chrysolite, whose rooms and doors, flowing rivers, lofty hanging fruits; therein is no grief nor sorrow. He said: So they are in need of nothing as they are of Friday, to increase in looking toward their Lord and increase in honor from Him."
"828","Abu Ya'la said: Shayban narrated to us, as-Sa'q ibn Hazn narrated, Ali ibn al-Hakam al-Bunani narrated, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), and he mentioned similar, and in it: And we call it with us the Day of Increase. I said: What is the Increase? He said: Allah made in Paradise a wide valley, and made in it dunes of musk; so when it is Friday He descends therein, and says: Clothe My servants, feed My servants, give drink to My servants, perfume My servants; then says: What do you want? They say: We want Your pleasure, our Lord. So He says: I am pleased with you. So they depart, and the wide-eyed maidens ascend to chambers of green chrysolite or red ruby. This is the end of the hadith from this route, and he did not mention what is after it; and its chain is better than the first."
"829","Al-Harith said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Sa'd narrated, from Aban, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The five prayers, and Jumu'a to Jumu'a, are expiations for what is between them, as long as major sins are avoided. So a man said: O Messenger of Allah, does Jumu'a indeed expiate to Jumu'a? He said: Yes, plus three days; and in it is an hour no Muslim servant agrees with, asking Allah therein good, except He gives it to him; and the days were shown to me, so I saw Friday, in it as if a mirror of beauty and light, so it pleased me; then I saw in it a black spot, so I asked Jibril (peace be upon him), and he said: It is the hour in which the Hour is established."
"830","Abu Ya'la said: Abdullah ibn Abd as-Samad ibn Ali narrated to us, my father narrated, from Awwam al-Basri, from Abd al-Wahid ibn Zayd, from Thabit, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The day of Jumu'a and the night of Jumu'a are twenty-four hours; there is no hour therein except Allah has therein six hundred freed from the Fire. He said: So we exited from him and entered upon al-Hasan, and mentioned to him the hadith of Thabit, so he said: I heard it, and added in it: All of them deserved the Fire."
"831","Muhammad ibn Bahr narrated to us, Yahya ibn Sulaym narrated, al-Azwar ibn Ghalib narrated, from Thabit and Sulayman at-Taymi, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), similar, but he said in it: Six hundred thousand freed, and said at its end: One of them said: All of them deserved the Fire."
"832","Ishaq ibn Rahawayh said: Abd ar-Rahman ibn Muhammad al-Muharibi told us, al-Asbagh ibn Zayd narrated, from Sa'id ibn Rashid, from Zayd ibn Ali, from Fatima, daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), she said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: In Jumu'a there is an hour no Muslim agrees with, supplicating with good, except he is answered. So Fatima said: O Messenger of Allah, which hour is it? He said: When the sun declines to setting. And Fatima used to say to a boy called Arbid: Ascend upon the hill; when you see the sun decline to setting, tell me; so he would tell her, and she would stand to her mosque and keep supplicating until the sun sets, then pray. I say: Zayd did not meet Fatima, and Sa'id ibn Rashid is weak."
"833","Ishaq said: Suwayd ibn Abd al-Aziz told us, Abu Nusayra al-Wasiti narrated, he said: I heard Abu Raja al-Utaridi narrate from Abu Bakr as-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: A bedouin came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: It reached me that you say: Jumu'a to Jumu'a, and the five prayers are expiations for what is between them, for one who avoids major sins? So he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes. Then he added to him and said: Bathing on Friday is expiation, and walking to Jumu'a is expiation: each foot thereof for the deed of twenty years; and when he finishes the Jumu'a prayer he is rewarded with the deed of two hundred years."
"834","Baqiyya told us, from ad-Dahhak ibn Humra, from Abu Nusayra, from Abu Raja, from Imran and Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with them both), they said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever bathes on Friday, his sins and faults are expiated; and when he begins walking, for him with each step is the deed of twenty years; and when he finishes Jumu'a he is rewarded with the deed of two hundred years. Ishaq said: Ad-Dahhak ibn Humra is trustworthy in hadith - Ahmad in az-Zuhd."
"835","Abu Ja'far ibn at-Tabba' narrated to us, Makhlad narrated, from Hisham, from Ibn Sirin, he said: He liked that no ritual impurity come between the Friday bath and the prayer, and says: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you comes to Jumu'a, let him bathe."
"836","Musaddad said: And Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Hassan, Qays ibn Sa'd told me, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: He was asked about the hour on Friday, so he said: Allah knows; Allah created Adam (peace be upon him) on Friday after Asr, from a handful He seized from the whole surface of the earth; do you not see that among his progeny are the red, the black, the wicked, and the good; then He covenanted to him and he forgot; thence man was named insan; by Allah, the sun of that day did not set until he was sent down to the world."
"837","Chapter: Upon whom Jumu'a is obligatory. Musaddad said: Abu Awana narrated to us, from Humayd at-Tawil, he said: Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) used to be in his palace; sometimes he would gather, and sometimes he would not gather."
"838","Yahya narrated to us, from Shu'ba, from Ata ibn Abi Maymuna, from Abu Rafi', from Abu Hurayra (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: We wrote to Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) asking him about Jumu'a in Bahrain, so he wrote to us: Gather wherever you are."
"839","Yahya narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Abi Aruba, from Qatada, from Kathir, freedman of Ibn Samura, he said: I passed by Abd ar-Rahman ibn Samura (may Allah be pleased with him) while he was sitting at his door on Friday, so he said: What is the matter with your amir? We said: Have you not heard? He said: No; this mud held us back."
"840","Hushaym narrated to us, Yunus narrated, from al-Hasan, he said: The blind, if he finds no guide, there is no Jumu'a upon him."
"841","Sulaym ibn Akhdar narrated to us, from Ibn Awn, he said: Abu al-Malih was over al-Ubulla, and there was none among al-Hajjaj's officials more God-fearing than Abu al-Malih; so when it was Friday he would come and gather with Basra, then return."
"842","Abu Bakr said: Surayj narrated to us, from al-Hakam ibn Abd al-Malik, from Qatada, from al-Hasan, from Samura (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Attend Jumu'a, and draw near the imam; for a man stays behind from Jumu'a though he is among its people."
"843","Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Shu'ba, Abu Ishaq narrated, Abu Amr ash-Shaybani narrated, he said: I saw Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) expelling the women on Friday from the mosque. A sound chain."
"844","Chapter: Bathing for Jumu'a. Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Hisham ibn Hassan told us, from Ibn Sirin, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), from Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: We were commanded with bathing on Friday. I said: You, the first Muhajirun, or the people in general? He said: I do not know. This is a good chain - if Ibn Sirin heard from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both)."
"845","And he also said: Hushaym narrated to us, Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad narrated, from Abdullah ibn al-Harith ibn Nawfal, he said: I heard Sa'd (may Allah be pleased with him) say: I did not think anyone leaves bathing on Friday."
"846","Abd ibn Humayd said: Malik ibn Ismail narrated to us, Mindal narrated, from Abdullah ibn Marwan, from Ba'ja, from his father, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: And he mentioned a hadith in it: Whoever bathes on Friday, it is as if he fasted a day in the path of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and the day is as seven hundred."
"847","Abu Bakr said: Ali ibn Hashim narrated to us, Ibn Abi Layla narrated, from Atiyya, from Abu Sa'id (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever purifies himself and perfects purification, then comes to Jumu'a, without engaging in idle diversion or ignorance, it is expiation for what is between it and the next Jumu'a; and the five prayers are expiation for what is between them; and in Jumu'a is an hour no Muslim servant agrees with, asking Allah therein good, except He gives him."
"848","Hushaym narrated to us, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, from Abu Hurayra (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: My close friend (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) enjoined upon me bathing on Friday."
"849","At-Tayalisi said: Al-Mas'udi narrated to us, from Wabara, from Hammam ibn al-Harith, he said: Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Bathing on Friday is among the Sunna."
"850","Al-Harith said: Al-Muqri narrated to us, al-Mas'udi narrated, with it."
"851","At-Tayalisi also said: Abu Hurra narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from Abd ar-Rahman ibn Samura (may Allah be pleased with him), and I know it only from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever performs ablution on Friday, then with it, and excellent; and whoever bathes, bathing is better. I say: The well-known from al-Hasan in this is from Samura ibn Jundub, not from Abd ar-Rahman ibn Samura."
"852","Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Shu'ba, from Amr ibn Murra, from Zadham, he said: A man asked Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) about bathing? So he said: Bathe every day if you wish. He said: No, but bathing - that is the recommended. He said: Bathe every Friday, the day of Fitr, the day of sacrifice, and the day of Arafa."
"853","Muhammad ibn Jabir narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: They liked to have intercourse on Friday to make bathing obligatory. And the hadith of Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) comes, Allah willing, in the prohibition of stepping over."
"854","Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Malik, he said: Az-Zuhri told me, from Ibn as-Sabbaq, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on one of the Fridays: This is a day of festival Allah made for the Muslims; so bathe, and whoever has perfume, it harms him not to touch of it, and upon you is the siwak."
"855","Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, from Muhammad ibn Abd ar-Rahman, from a man from the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from the Ansar (may Allah be pleased with them), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: It is a duty upon every Muslim to bathe on Friday, to use the tooth-stick, and to touch of perfume if he has."
"856","Chapter: The time of Jumu'a. Al-Harith said: Muhammad ibn Umar narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Ismail ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'd narrated, from his father, from Amir ibn Sa'd, from his father (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray Jumu'a when the sun declines."
"857","Abu Ya'la said: Zuhayr narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated, Sufyan ibn Husayn narrated, from al-Hakam ibn Utayba, he said: A man delayed the prayer on Friday, so an elder said to him: By Allah, I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pray, and I did not see him do as you do! He said: When I saw him mention the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - I said to him: How did you see him do? He said: I saw him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - exit when the sun declined; and behold the man was Abu Juhayfa (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"858","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Sufyan narrated to us, from az-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both): That he went early to Jumu'a, and when the sun declined Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) came out to them and sat upon the pulpit; so the caller began his call; when he fell silent he stood, praised Allah and extolled Him. This is a sound chain."
"859","Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Shu'ba, he said: Salama ibn Kuhayl told me, from Mus'ab ibn Sa'd, he said: Sa'd (may Allah be pleased with him) used to take midday rest after Jumu'a. Sound."
"860","Yahya narrated to us, from Shu'ba, he said: Khubayb ibn Abd ar-Rahman told me, from his aunt Unaysa (may Allah be pleased with her) - and she had performed Hajj with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - she said: Our men used to gather with Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), then return with their cloaks upon their heads, following the shade of the walls, taking midday rest after it. Its chain is sound."
"861","Chapter: Etiquettes of the sermon. Abu Ya'la said: Ibrahim ibn Dinar narrated to us, Mus'ab ibn Sallam narrated, from Hamza az-Zayyat, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Bara (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us until he made the maidens in their chambers hear... the hadith."
"862","Abu Bakr said: Al-Muharibi narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj, from al-Hakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): That he used to address on Friday standing, then sit, then stand and address."
"863","Abu Ya'la said: It was read to Bishr - meaning Ibn al-Walid -: Abu Yusuf told you, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Hajjaj, with it, and added: So he sat a light sitting."
"864","Al-Harith said: Muhammad ibn Umar al-Waqidi narrated to us, Sulayman ibn al-Harith al-Ansari narrated, from Abbas ibn Sahl, from his father."
"865","And Abdullah ibn Yazid al-Bahili, from Iyas ibn Salama, from his father."
"866","And Umar ibn Salih, from Abu Usayd ibn Ali, from Abu Humayd as-Sa'idi. And Muhammad ibn Nu'aym, from his father, from Abu Hurayra (may Allah be pleased with them): All of them from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): That he used to address two sermons, and sit two sittings: when he first ascends, and between the two sermons."
"867","Abd ibn Humayd said: Ibrahim ibn al-Hakam narrated to us, my father narrated, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited in his sermon al-Ma'ida and Surat at-Tawba."
"868","Chapter: Taking the pulpit. Ishaq said: Uqba ibn Khalid as-Sakuni told us, Musa ibn Muhammad ibn Ibrahim at-Taymi narrated, from his father, from as-Saluli, from Mu'adh ibn Jabal (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: If I take a pulpit, my father Ibrahim (peace be upon him) took one; and if I take the staff, my father Ibrahim (peace be upon him) took one."
"869","Al-Bazzar narrated it, from Abu Sa'id al-Ashajj, from Uqba, and said: We do not know it except with this chain."
"870","Abu Bakr said: Khalid ibn Makhlad narrated to us, from Musa ibn Ya'qub, Abu Hazim told me, Sahl ibn Sa'd told me, he said: The wood in the chamber: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to lean upon it when he stood; when he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away it was stolen, so it was sought and found in the mosque of Banu Amr ibn Awf, while the termite had hollowed it; so two pieces of wood were carved for it and hollowed, then closed upon it, then the two pieces were mended upon it; so when you see it you see the mend in it."
"871","Abd said: Ali ibn Asim informed us, from al-Jurayri, from Abu Nadrah al-Abdi; Abu Said al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to me, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to deliver the Friday sermon leaning against a palm trunk. It was said to him: O Messenger of Allah, the people have grown many, and they love to see you; if only you would take a pulpit to stand upon so the people can see you. He said: Yes, who will make this pulpit for us? A man stood up to him and said: I will. He said: Will you make it? He said: Yes - and he did not say: if Allah wills. He said: What is your name? He said: So-and-so. He said: Sit down, so he sat. Then he repeated: Who will make this pulpit for us? A man stood up and said: I will. He said: Will you make it? He said: Yes, and he did not say: if Allah wills. He said: What is your name? He said: So-and-so. He said: Sit down, so he sat. Then he repeated: Who will make this pulpit for us? A man stood up and said: I will. He said: Will you make it? He said: Yes, if Allah wills. He said: What is your name? He said: Ibrahim. He said: Make it. When Friday came and the people gathered, and he ascended the pulpit and stood upon it facing the people, the palm tree moaned, until it made me hear though I was at the far end of the mosque. He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came down from the pulpit and embraced it, and did not cease until it calmed. Then he returned to the pulpit, praised Allah the Exalted and extolled Him, then said: This palm tree only moaned out of longing for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he left it; by Allah, had I not come down to it and embraced it, it would not have calmed until the Day of Resurrection."
"872","Abu Bakr said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, from Mujalid, from Abu al-Waddak, from Abu Said (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to sermonize leaning against a trunk. A Roman man came to him and said: Shall I make for you a pulpit upon which you sermonize? So he made for him this one which you see. When he stood upon it to sermonize, the trunk moaned like the moaning of a she-camel for its calf. He came down to it and pressed it to himself, and it calmed. Then he ordered that a hole be dug for it and it be buried."
"873","Abu Yala narrated it: from Masruq ibn al-Marzuban, from Yahya ibn Abi Zaidah, from Mujalid, and he said at its end: The next day I saw it had been moved, so I said: What is this? They said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came with Abu Bakr and Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) and moved it."
"874","With the same chain, from Ibn Abi Zaidah, from Abu Ishaq, from Said ibn Abi Karib, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him), and he mentioned the like with addition."
"875","Chapter: The command to adorn oneself on Friday. 709 - Abu Bakr said: Ibn Numayr narrated to us, from Musa ibn Ubaydah, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked at the people on Friday with their appearance unkempt, and said: What harm would it do a man to take two garments for this day? Before the next Friday came, garments arrived from Bahrain, coarse ones; so there was the one with two garments and the one with a striped cloak. Musa is weak."
"876","710 - Al-Harith said: Muhammad ibn Umar narrated to us; Abdullah ibn Abi Yahya narrated to us, from Said ibn Abi Hind, from Dhakwan Abu Amr, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had two garments which he would wear on Friday; when he returned he would fold them and put them away."
"877","711 - Musaddad: Hafs narrated to us; al-Hajjaj narrated to us, from Abu Jafar, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him): that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to wear his red cloak on the two Eids and on Friday. Hajjaj is weak."
"878","Chapter: Keeping silent during the sermon. 712 - Ishaq said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi informed us; Abdullah ibn Jafar - of the offspring of al-Miswar - narrated to me, from Ismail ibn Muhammad ibn Sad, he informed him, from al-Saib ibn Yazid, he said: We used to pray in the time of Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) on Friday; when Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) came out and sat upon the pulpit we stopped praying, and we would talk and he would talk to us; a man might ask the one next to him about his market and his servants. When the caller to prayer fell silent, he sermonized, and we would not speak until he finished his sermon. This is a sound chain, halted."
"879","713 - Abu Bakr said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, from Mujalid, from Amir, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: Sad ibn Abi Waqqas (may Allah be pleased with him) said to a man on a Friday: There is no Friday for you. The man mentioned that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, Sad said to me: There is no Friday for you. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Why, O Sad? He said: He spoke while you were sermonizing. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Sad spoke the truth. Its chain is close."
"880","714 - Abu Yala said: Abd al-Ala narrated to us; Yaqub narrated to us; Isa ibn Jariyah narrated to me, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: Abdullah ibn Masud (may Allah be pleased with him) entered the mosque while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was sermonizing, and sat beside Ubayy ibn Kab (may Allah be pleased with him) and asked him about something, but he did not answer him. He thought it was anger, and mentioned that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Ubayy (may Allah be pleased with him) said: O Abdullah, you were not present with us for Friday. He said: Why? He said: You spoke while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was sermonizing. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Ubayy spoke the truth. And the hadith of Abu Said (may Allah be pleased with him) has preceded in the chapter of bathing."
"881","Chapter: The sermon of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 715 - Al-Harith said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us; Maysarah ibn Abd Rabbihi narrated to us, from Abu Aishah al-Sadi, from Yazid ibn Umar, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah and Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them), they said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sermonized to us, and he mentioned the hadith at length - and in it: Then he sermonized and said: Praise be to Allah; we praise Him, seek His help, seek His forgiveness, believe in Him and rely upon Him; and we bear witness that there is no god but Allah, alone without partner, and that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger; and we seek refuge in Allah from the evils of our souls and the evil of our deeds. Whom Allah guides, none can misguide; and whom He misguides, none can guide."
"882","Chapter: Greeting the mosque while the imam is sermonizing. 716 - Al-Humaydi said: Sufyan narrated to us; Hassan ibn Jadarah narrated to us, he said: I saw al-Hasan ibn Abi al-Hasan (may Allah have mercy on him) enter the mosque of Wasit on Friday while Ibn Hubayrah was sermonizing upon the pulpit, and he prayed two rakahs, then sat."
"883","Chapter: Warning against staying away from Friday. 717 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Shubah, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his uncle (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever abandons Friday three times, his heart is sealed, and his heart is made like the heart of a hypocrite."
"884","718 - Abu Yala said: Humayd ibn Masadah narrated to us, from Sufyan ibn Habib, from Awf, from Said ibn Abi al-Hasan, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: Whoever abandons the Friday prayer three times in succession has cast Islam behind his back."
"885","719 - Sufyan, he is Ibn Waki, narrated to us; my father narrated to us, from Said ibn Ubayd; al-Fadl al-Raqashi narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood to sermonize on Friday and said: Perhaps a man is only a mile from Medina and Friday comes upon him, yet he does not attend it. Then he said the second time: while he is two miles from Medina and does not attend it. Then he said the third time: while he is three miles from Medina and does not attend Friday, and Allah seals his heart."
"886","Chapter: The prohibition of stepping over the necks of people on Friday. 720 - Ibn Abi Umar said: Bishr ibn al-Sari narrated to us; Umar ibn al-Walid al-Shanni narrated to us, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: A man came while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was sermonizing on Friday, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: One of you idles until Friday is almost missed, then he comes stepping over the necks of people, harming them. He said: I did not do so, O Prophet of Allah; I was asleep, then I awoke, stood, performed ablution, then came. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Is this a day for ablution?! Its men are trustworthy, except Umar, about whom there is criticism."
"887","Chapter: Whoever catches a rakah of Friday has caught it. 721 - Musaddad said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from al-Ahwas ibn Hakim, from Rashid ibn Sad (may Allah have mercy on them), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever catches a rakah of Friday, let him add another to it. This is sent forth."
"888","722 / 1 - Ibn Abi Umar said: Waki narrated to us; Yasin al-Zayyat narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Said and Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever catches a rakah of Friday, let him pray another with it; and whoever misses both rakahs, let him pray four, or the noon prayer, or the first one."
"889","722 / 2 - Abu Yala said: It was recited to Bishr, he is Ibn al-Walid: Abu Yusuf informed you, from al-Hajjaj, from al-Zuhri with it. And its wording: Whoever catches a rakah of Friday prays another with it."
"890","722 / 3 - Al-Hakim narrated it by way of Salih ibn Abi al-Akhdar, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), and he mentioned the like of the narration of Yasin al-Zayyat, and its wording: If he catches them sitting, he prays four."
"891","Chapter: Who prayed after Friday and who disliked that. 723 - Musaddad: Yahya narrated to us; Abu Amir Salih ibn Rustum narrated to us; Humayd ibn Hilal narrated to us, from al-Hakam ibn al-Araj, or Husayn ibn Abi al-Hurr, he said: I saw Imran ibn Husayn (may Allah be pleased with him) pray Friday, then pray two rakahs after it. They said: He completed it... he completed it. I mentioned that to Imran (may Allah be pleased with him), and he said: For spears to pierce my belly is dearer to me than to do that deliberately. I watched him on the second Friday: he prayed, then sat with his knees drawn up and did not pray until he stood for Asr."
"892","724 / 1 - He said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi informed us; Zuhayr ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil ibn Abi Talib, from Amr ibn Shurahbil ibn Said ibn Sad ibn Ubadah, from his father, from his grandfather Sad ibn Ubadah: that a man said: O Messenger of Allah, tell us about Friday, what good is in it? He said: In it are five qualities: In it Allah created Adam, and in it Allah sent Adam down, and in it Allah took Adam in death; and in it is an hour in which no Muslim supplicates but Allah grants it to him, so long as he does not ask for a sin or severing of kinship; and in it the Hour will come. There is no close angel, nor earth, nor mountains, nor wind, but it fears Friday."
"893","724 / 2 - Zakariya ibn Adi informed us, from Abdullah ibn Amr, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil with it. This is a good hadith, if Shurahbil heard from his grandfather Sad ibn Ubadah."
"894","Chapter: Shortening the prayer while traveling, and combining between prayers. 725 - Musaddad: Yahya narrated to us, from Shubah, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damrah, he said: Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) prayed Asr while traveling as two rakahs, then entered his tent and prayed two rakahs, while I looked on."
"895","726 / 1 - Abu Muawiyah narrated to us; al-Hajjaj narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: We prayed the travel prayer with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as two rakahs each, except Maghrib as three."
"896","726 / 2 - Likewise Muhammad ibn Abi Umar, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah and Ahmad ibn Mani, all three, said it from Abu Muawiyah, and in their narration: Except Maghrib, for he prayed it as three."
"897","727 / 1 - Musaddad said: Abd al-Wahid narrated to us, from al-Mughirah ibn Ziyad, from Ata, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would complete the prayer and shorten it, meaning while traveling."
"898","727 / 2 - Abu Yala said: Abd al-Ala narrated to us; Abdullah ibn Dawud narrated to us; al-Mughirah narrated with it. And he added: He would delay Zuhr and hasten Asr, and delay Maghrib and hasten Isha."
"899","728 - Ahmad ibn Mani said: Yazid narrated to us; al-Awwam ibn Hawshab narrated to us; al-Qasim ibn Awf narrated to me, from a man, he said: I came to Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him) at al-Baldah, which is Mina, and it was said to him: Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) prayed four. He said: I prayed with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Abu Bakr and Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), and they prayed two rakahs; then he stood and prayed four."
"900","729 - Ibn Abi Umar said: Waki narrated to us; Said ibn Ubayd narrated to us, from Ali ibn Rabiah, from al-Rabi ibn Nadlah, he said: We went out on a journey, twelve riders, all of whom had accompanied Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except me. The prayer came, a man from the people stepped forward and prayed four. Salman (may Allah be pleased with him) said: What have we to do with four; half of four suffices us; we are in greater need of lightening."
"901","730 / 1 - Abu Dawud said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Qays, he said: I heard al-Huzayl, he is Ibn Shurahbil, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was on a journey; he delayed Zuhr and hastened Asr, combining them, and delayed Maghrib and hastened Isha, combining them. Shubah did not say: from Abdullah; and it was narrated that Ibn Abi Layla joined it from Abdullah."
"902","730 / 2 - Abu Bakr said: Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us; Isa narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Abu Qays al-Awdi, from Ibn Masud (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to combine between the two prayers while traveling."
"903","731 - Ishaq said: Umar ibn Hafs informed us; my father narrated to me, he said: I witnessed al-Numan ibn Bashir combine Maghrib and Isha."
"904","732 - Musaddad: Hushaym narrated to us, from Mansur ibn Zadham, from Bakr, he is Ibn Abdullah al-Muzani, he said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote to Abu Musa (may Allah be pleased with him): that combining two prayers without excuse is among the major sins. This is halted, broken between Bakr and Abu Musa (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"905","Ishaq said: I said to Abu Usamah: Did Sad ibn Said al-Ansari narrate to you, he said: I heard al-Saib ibn Yazid say: The prayer was obligated as two prostrations... two prostrations, Zuhr and Asr; they would pray two rakahs after Zuhr and two after Asr. Then Zuhr and Asr were written upon them as four, and they left that when it was written upon them, and the travel prayer was left as decreed, and the resident prayer was four? So he affirmed it and said: Yes. This is a good hadith."
"906","734 - Musaddad said: Marwan ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, from Humayd ibn Ali al-Uqayli, from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: Whoever prays four while traveling is like one who prays two in residence. This is halted, weak."
"907","735 / 1 - Abd al-Warith narrated to us, from Abu Harun al-Abdi, from Abu Said al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left Medina and traveled a farsakh, he shortened the prayer."
"908","735 / 2 - Abu Bakr said: Hushaym narrated to us; Abu Harun informed us with it."
"909","735 / 3 - Ahmad ibn Mani said: Hushaym narrated to us with it."
"910","735 / 4 - Abd said: Ali ibn Asim narrated to us; Abu Harun narrated to us with it, and its wording: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); when he passed a farsakh he shortened the prayer. Abu Harun is weak."
"911","736 - Abd said: Abd al-Razzaq informed us; Mamar informed us, from Qatadah, from Muwarriq al-Ijli, he said: Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) was asked about prayer while traveling, and said: Two rakahs... two rakahs; whoever opposes the Sunnah disbelieves. Its chain is sound."
"912","737 - Al-Tayalisi said: Abdullah ibn Badr narrated to us, from Sawwar ibn Shabib, he said: I asked Abdullah ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) about prayer while traveling, and he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Two rakahs... two rakahs, except Maghrib."
"913","738 - Musaddad said: Mutamir narrated to us, from Ubaydullah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both): that he would go out to al-Ghabah and neither break fast nor shorten."
"914","739 - Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj; Ata informed me: that a man asked Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both): Shall I shorten to Arafah? He said: Do not shorten the prayer except for a full day's journey."
"915","740 - Yahya narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik, from Ata, from Abu Mabad, he said: We were with Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) on a journey; the sun set, and it was said to him: O Abu al-Abbas, the prayer? He said: We are traveling people. Then he went on until he came to Marr al-Zahran and stopped; between where it was said to him: the prayer, when the sun set, and where he stopped was two farsakhs."
"916","Chapter: The fear prayer. 741 - Ahmad ibn Mani, Musaddad and Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah, all said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us; al-Hajjaj narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I prayed the fear prayer with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as two rakahs, except Maghrib as three."
"917","742 - Abu Bakr said: Muhammad ibn Bishr narrated to us; Said narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu al-Aliyah al-Riyahi, he said: Abu Musa (may Allah be pleased with him) was at al-Dar in Isfahan, and there was no great fear there that day, but he wished to teach them their religion and the Sunnah of their Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He made them two rows: a group with weapons facing the enemy, and a group before him. He prayed one rakah with those near him, then they stepped back until they stood in the place of the others, threading through them, until they stood behind him; then he prayed another rakah with them, then gave salutation. Then those near him and the others stood and prayed a rakah each, then gave salutation to one another. The imam's prayer was completed as two rakahs in congregation, and the people's as a rakah each. Its men are trustworthy, except there is a break between Abu al-Aliyah and Abu Musa (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"918","743 - Abu Dawud said: Sharik narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Sulaym ibn Abd, from Hudhayfah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The fear prayer is two rakahs and four prostrations; if a matter hastens him, fighting and speech are permitted."
"919","Chapter: The eclipse prayer. 744 - Abu Bakr said: Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us, from Yazid, he is Ibn Abi Ziyad, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, he said: So-and-so and so-and-so narrated to me, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The sun and the moon are two signs among the signs of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic; when you see that, hasten to prayer."
"920","745 - Musaddad said: Ismail narrated to us; Khalid narrated to us, from Abu Qilabah, he said: The sun eclipsed in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Pray as the most recent obligatory prayer you prayed."
"921","Abu Yala said: Abu Khaythamah narrated to us; al-Hasan ibn Musa narrated to us; Ibn Lahiah narrated to us; Yazid ibn Abi Habib narrated to us, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: I prayed the eclipse prayer behind the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I did not hear a letter from him therein."
"922","Chapter: The prayer for rain. 747 - Abu Dawud said: Imran al-Qattan narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Nasr ibn Asim, from Muawiyah al-Laythi (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: People wake up stricken with drought, then Allah brings them provision from Himself, and they wake up as polytheists; they say: We were rained upon by such-and-such a star."
"923","748 - Abu Yala said: Abu Khaythamah narrated to us; Waki narrated to us, from al-Rabi ibn Subayh, from Yazid ibn Aban, he is al-Raqashi, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would expose himself to the first of his rain, removing his clothes except the waist-wrapper."
"924","749 - Abu Bakr said: Muhammad ibn al-Qasim narrated to us; Fitr narrated to us, from Abu Khalid al-Walibi, from Jabir ibn Samurah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Three things I fear for my community: seeking rain by the stars, the tyranny of the ruler, and denial of the decree."
"925","750 / 1 - Abu Yala said: Abu Kurayb narrated to us; al-Hasan ibn Atiyah narrated to us; Qays narrated to us, from Yunus ibn Ubayd, from al-Hasan, from al-Ahnaf, from al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from Medina, and he turned to it and said: Allah, the Blessed and Exalted, has cleared this peninsula of polytheism, but I fear for them that the stars will misguide them. They said: O Messenger of Allah, how will the stars misguide them? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Rain descends, and they say: We were rained upon by such-and-such a star."
"926","750 / 2 - Abu Yala said: Musa ibn Muhammad narrated to us; Abd al-Samad narrated to us; Umar ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah the Exalted has purified this town of polytheism, unless the stars misguide them."
"927","Chapter: The prayer of the two Eids. 751 - Ishaq said: al-Mutamir ibn Sulayman informed us; Qurrah ibn Abi al-Sahba informed me, from al-Ala ibn Badr, he said: Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) went out on a day of Eid and saw people praying, and said: O people, we witnessed the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the like of this day, and none would pray before the Eid, or before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). A man said: O Commander of the Faithful, will you not forbid praying before the imam comes out? He said: I do not wish to forbid a servant when he prays, but we tell them what we witnessed of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I say: Al-Bazzar narrated it by way of Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn al-Numan al-Jufi Abu Ishaq, he said: I heard al-Rabi ibn Said al-Jufi; al-Walid ibn Sari, freedman of Amr ibn Hurayth, narrated to us with it."
"928","752 - Ishaq said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa informed us; Khalid ibn Ilyas narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Hatib, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would come to the Eid walking. Another added in it, from his father, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) come to the Eid walking, returning by one road and taking another. This is a weak chain because of Khalid. It has a witness in al-Bukhari from the hadith of Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"929","753 - Al-Harith said: Yazid narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us; I said to Nafi: How did Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) pray on the day of Eid? He said: He would attend the dawn prayer with the imam, then return to his house and bathe as from major impurity, wear his finest clothes, perfume himself with the finest he had, then go out until he came to the prayer ground, and sit in it until the imam came; when the imam came he prayed with him, then returned and entered the mosque of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and prayed two rakahs in it, then came to his house."
"930","754 - Musaddad said: Sufyan narrated to us: that he heard Amr ibn Dinar narrate that Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), on the day of departure, would utter the takbir and order those around him to utter it, acting upon His Exalted saying: When you have completed your rites, remember Allah."
"931","755 - Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Ajlan; Nafi narrated to me that Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) would go early to the Eid from the mosque, raising his voice with the takbir until he came to the prayer ground, and utter the takbir until the imam came."
"932","756 - Ahmad ibn Mani said: Ibn Ulayyah narrated to us; al-Layth narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Hanash, he said: It was said to Ali (may Allah be pleased with him): People are unable to go out; among them is one with an ailment, and among them is one for whom the mosque is far. He (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Pray here and in the mosque, and pray four: two rakahs for the Sunnah, and two for the going out."
"933","757 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from al-Hakam ibn Farrukh, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both): that he would utter the takbir from the morning of Arafah until the end of the days of Tashriq, and would not utter it in Maghrib; and his takbir was: Allah is Most Great, greatly; Allah is Most Great, greatly; Allah is Most Great, greatly; and praise be to Allah; Allah is Most Great for having guided us. Ahmad narrated it from Yahya, abridged."
"934","758 - Musaddad said: Hafs narrated to us, from Hajjaj, from Abu Jafar, from Jabir: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would wear his red cloak on the two Eids and on Friday. Hajjaj is weak."
"935","759 - Musaddad said: Sulaym ibn Akhdar narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, he said: Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) would not eat on the day of Fitr until he returned from the prayer ground."
"936","760 - Al-Harith said: Muhammad ibn Umar narrated to us; Musa ibn Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Taymi narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Abi Hakim, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz (may Allah have mercy on him): that he distributed dates among his companions before going early to the Eid, and said: Eat before you go early. Ibrahim ibn Abi Qariz narrated to us, from Abu Said (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah and his family be upon him) would do it. Ahmad brought it from the hadith of Ibn Aqil, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Said (may Allah be pleased with him), the like of it."
"937","761 - Musaddad said: Abdullah narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from Wahb ibn Kaysan, from a man, he said: Abu Bakr and Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) would pray the Eid before the sermon."
"938","762 - Abu Bakr said: al-Fadl ibn Dukayn narrated to us; Zuhayr narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq; a man narrated to me: that he saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pray on the day of Fitr between the two stones. Abu Ishaq said: Where food is sold."
"939","763 - Ahmad ibn Mani said: Yazid narrated to us; Humayd informed us, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The prayer on the two Eids was before the sermon."
"940","764 / 1 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj; Ata narrated to us, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: The takbir in Fitr is one takbir with which the prayer is opened, then he utters five, then recites, then utters the takbir and bows, then stands and utters five, then recites, then utters the takbir and bows."
"941","764 / 2 - Al-Harith said: Yazid narrated to us; Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman narrated to us, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas."
"942","764 / 3 - And Yazid, from Humayd, from Ammar ibn Abi Ammar, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), the like of it."
"943","765 - He said: And Abdullah ibn Awn narrated to us; Faraj ibn Fadalah narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Amir al-Aslami, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would utter seven takbirs in the first of the Eid, and five in the last."
"944","766 - Abu Dawud said: Umarah ibn Zadham narrated to us, he said: We were with Thabit al-Bunani, and an elder was with him; we mentioned what is recited in the two Eids, and the elder said: I accompanied Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) to al-Zawiyah on a day of Eid, and a freedman of theirs was leading them in prayer; he recited: Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High, and: By the night when it covers. Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) said: He recited the two surahs which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited."
"945","767 - Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, from Sad ibn Ishaq, from his uncle, he said: I went out with Kab ibn Ujrah (may Allah be pleased with him) on the day of Eid, and he did not pray before it; when we had prayed, he saw the people all going to the mosque, and said: This is an innovation and abandoning the Sunnah."
"946","768 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Shubah, from Ata ibn al-Saib, he said: Maysarah would pray before the imam on the day of Eid, so I said: Did Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) not dislike prayer before it? He said: Yes."
"947","769 - Abu Yala said: Abu al-Rabi narrated to us; Hammad narrated to us; Ayyub narrated to us, he said: I saw Anas ibn Malik and al-Hasan (may Allah be pleased with them both) pray on the day of Eid before the imam came out. He said: And I saw Muhammad ibn Sirin come and sit, and not pray."
"948","Book of Funerals. Chapter: The states of the dying. 770 - Musaddad said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Husayn, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah (may Allah have mercy on him): that he willed: When I am attended, seat beside me one who prompts me: There is no god but Allah; hasten me to my pit, and do not announce me to the people, for I fear that is wailing like the wailing of the pre-Islamic age. Halted upon Alqamah, sound."
"949","771 - Abu Yala said: Ubaydullah ibn Umar narrated to us; Zaidah ibn Abi al-Ruqad narrated to us, from Ziyad al-Numayri, from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) entered upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was gloomy, and said: Why do I see you gloomy? He said: O Messenger of Allah, I was with a cousin of mine last night, so-and-so, while he was struggling with his soul. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Did you not prompt him: There is no god but Allah? He said: I did, O Messenger of Allah. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Did he say it? He said: Yes. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Paradise has become obligatory for him. Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: O Messenger of Allah, how is it for the living? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It demolishes their sins; it demolishes their sins."
"950","772 - Abu Yala said: Ishaq ibn Abi Israil narrated to us; Faraj ibn Fadalah narrated to us, from al-Ala ibn al-Harith, from Makhul, he said: Muadh ibn Jabal (may Allah be pleased with him) fell ill, and his companions came to visit him; he said: Seat me, so they seated him, and he said: A word I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever's last speech at death is: There is no god but Allah, alone without partner, it demolishes what was before it of sins and faults; so prompt your dying with it. They said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, how is it for the living? He said: More demolishing, more demolishing."
"951","773 - Abu Yala said: al-Abbas ibn al-Walid narrated to us; Wuhayb narrated to us, from Hisham, from his father, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: I entered upon Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) and saw death in him, and said: Agitation, agitation. He (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Do not say that, but say: And the stupor of death comes with the truth; that is what you used to avoid."
"952","774 / 1 - Abu Bakr said: Waki narrated to us, from al-Rabi ibn Said, he is al-Jufi, from Ibn Sabit, he is Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Sabit, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Narrate from the Children of Israel, for there were wonders among them. Then he began to narrate, he said: A group of them went out and came to one of their graveyards, and said: If only we prayed two rakahs and supplicated Allah the Exalted to bring out to us one of the dead to tell us about death? He said: So they did; while they were thus, a man poked his head out of an Abyssinian grave, with the trace of prostration between his eyes, and said: O people, what did you want from me? By Allah, I died a hundred years ago, and the heat of death has not calmed from me until now; so supplicate Allah the Exalted to return me as I was."
"953","774 / 2 - Abd narrated it, from Abu Bakr."
"954","774 / 3 - Ahmad ibn Mani said: Marwan ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, from Rabi ibn Hassan al-Jufi, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Sabit (may Allah have mercy on him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Narrate from the Children of Israel, and there is no harm, for there were wonders among them. He said: And Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to us in that gathering that a people of the Children of Israel went out walking in the land and reflecting upon it, and passed by a graveyard, and he mentioned it; and in it: A man came out to them with the trace of prostration between his eyes, black or Abyssinian, one of the two; and in it: What did you want from me? You have burdened me with a grave matter; by Allah, I have felt the taste of death and its heat for forty years, and your supplication coincided with its calming from me; so supplicate Allah the Exalted to return me as I was, and they supplicated, and He returned him as he was."
"955","775 - Ibn Abi Umar said: Marwan al-Fazari narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Qays al-Asadi, from Salm ibn Atiyah al-Fuqaymi, he said: Salman (may Allah be pleased with him) visited a sick man and saw him severe in his death-throes, and said: O Angel of Death, be gentle with him, for he is a believer. The man said: He says: I am gentle with every believer."
"956","776 - Al-Harith said: al-Hasan ibn Qutaybah narrated to us; Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Rawwad narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar (may Allah have mercy on him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The wrestling of the Angel of Death is severer than a thousand strikes with the sword, and no believer dies but every vein of him aches separately. Al-Harith said: I think he said: And give him glad tidings of Paradise, for the distress is great, the terror severe, and the closest the enemy of Allah is to him is that hour."
"957","777 - Ahmad ibn Mani said: Musa ibn Dawud narrated to us; Husam, he is Ibn Misakk, narrated to us, from Abu Mashar, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah: that he raided Khurasan and stayed two years praying two rakahs and not combining; the death of a cousin of his attended, and he went to visit him, and said: Ibn Masud (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no believer but he has sins requited in this world, and a remainder is left intensified upon him at death; and I do not like a death like the death of the donkey, meaning suddenness."
"958","778 - With this chain: The death of the believer is with the sweat of the forehead."
"959","779 / 1 - He also said: Ibn Ulayyah narrated to us, from Yunus, from Abu Mashar, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah; Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: The death of the believer is the sweat of the forehead; the believer has sins of his sins left, requited therewith at death, so his forehead sweats therefrom."
"960","779 / 2 - Musaddad said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us; Yunus narrated to us, the like of it."
"961","780 - Al-Bazzar said: Abdullah ibn al-Sabbah al-Attar narrated to us; Hajjaj ibn Nusayr narrated to us; al-Qasim ibn Mutayyab narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The death of the believer is with the sweat of the forehead."
"962","781 / 1 - Abd ibn Humayd said; Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us; Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Ayyub, from Bakr ibn Amr, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad ibn Anum, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Hubuli, from Abdullah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with them both), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The gift of the believer is death."
"963","781 / 2 - Abu Yala said: Abu Hamman narrated to us; Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us; Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us with it."
"964","782 - Ibn Abi Umar said: Abd al-Majid ibn Abi Rawwad narrated to us, from Marwan ibn Salim, from Safwan ibn Amr, from Shurayh ibn Ubayd, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No dead person dies with Ya Sin recited beside him but Allah the Exalted eases him."
"965","Chapter: The command of patience. 783 - Abu Yala said: Salih ibn Malik narrated to us; Abu Ubaydah al-Naji narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Sirin narrated to us, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a woman at al-Baqi crouching upon a grave, weeping, and said (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): O bondwoman of Allah, fear Allah and be patient. She said: O servant of Allah, I am the grieving, bereaved one. He said: O bondwoman of Allah, fear Allah and be patient. She said: O servant of Allah, I have heard; turn away from me. He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went on, and a man of his companions followed him and stopped by the woman and said to her: What did the departing man say to you? She said: He said to me such-and-such. He said: Do you know him? She said: No. He said: That is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). She leapt up hastening, saying: I am patient, I am patient, O Messenger of Allah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Patience is at the first shock; patience is at the first shock. This is a good hadith, for Abu Ubaydah, though criticized, the origin has a strong witness from the hadith of Anas (may Allah be pleased with him). Al-Bukhari and others brought it."
"966","Chapter: The reward of one whose child dies. 784 - Ahmad ibn Mani said: al-Hasan ibn Sawwar narrated to us; al-Layth narrated to us, from Shaybah al-Qurashi, from a man, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever expects the reward of three of his children who did not reach puberty, Allah the Exalted admits him to Paradise by His mercy toward them."
"967","785 - Abu Bakr said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir; al-Qasim narrated to us, from Abu Umamah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No two believers lose three children who did not reach puberty but Allah the Exalted admits them to Paradise by His mercy toward them."
"968","786 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Musa al-Juhani, from Mujahid, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: Whoever sends forth three of his children, patient and expecting reward, they veil him by Allah the Exalted's permission from the Fire. This is halted, good."
"969","787 - He also said: Maslamah narrated to us, from Dawud, from Abu Harb ibn Abi al-Aswad, from Mutarrif ibn Abdullah ibn al-Shikhkhir, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the Ansar (may Allah be pleased with them): What is raqub among you? They said: One who has no child. He said: That is not raqub; raqub is one who comes to his Lord, the Mighty and Majestic, without having sent forth any of his children. This is sent forth, strong."
"970","788 - Abu Yala said: Said ibn Abi al-Rabi narrated to us; Rushayd Abu Abdullah narrated to us; Thabit narrated to us, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stopped by a gathering of Bani Salimah and said: O Bani Salimah, what is raqub? And he mentioned the like of it."
"971","789 / 1 - Abu Bakr said: Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us; Isa ibn al-Mukhtar narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Abdullah ibn Ata al-Makki, from a man of the Ansar of Bani Zurayq, from Umm Mubashshir (may Allah be pleased with her), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): that he entered upon her while she was cooking hays, and said (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Whose three die without reaching puberty, they are a veil for him from the Fire. She said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, and two? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Three. I said: And two? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Three. Then he fell silent, then said (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Two, O Umm Mubashshir; two, O Umm Mubashshir."
"972","789 / 2 - Abu Yala said: Abu Bakr narrated to us with this."
"973","789 / 3 - And Ibn Ammar narrated to us; Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us; al-Muthanna narrated to us, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from Said ibn al-Musayyab, from Umm Mubashshir, and he mentioned the like of it, abridged."
"974","790 / 1 - Abu Bakr said: Abu Khalid narrated to us, from Hisham, from Ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whom do you count raqub among you? They said: One who has no child. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No, rather one who has no farat preceding him."
"975","790 / 2 - Abu Yala said: Abu Bakr narrated to us with this."
"976","790 / 3 - And Abu Hisham al-Rifai narrated to us; Abu Khalid narrated to us with it."
"977","791 - Abu Yala said: Salih ibn Malik narrated to us; Abu Ubaydah al-Naji narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Sirin narrated to us, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: A woman came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a sick son of hers, and said: O Messenger of Allah, supplicate Allah the Exalted to heal this son of mine. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do you have a farat? She said: Rather, in Islam. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A strong shield; a strong shield. This is a good chain, for Abu Ubaydah, though criticized, it came by a sound way from Abu Zurah, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"978","792 - Abu Yala said: Qasim ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us; Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq, from Yazid ibn al-Hakam, from Uthman ibn Abi al-As (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He has taken a strong shield who has three children precede him in Islam."
"979","793 - Abu Yala said: Abu Hisham al-Rifai narrated to us; Jafar ibn Awn narrated to us; Bashir ibn al-Muhajir narrated to us, from Abu Buraydah, from his father (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would tend the Ansar, visit their sick, and ask about them. It reached us that a woman of the Ansar lost a son and grieved over him, so he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to her and ordered her to be mindful of Allah the Exalted and be patient. She said: O Messenger of Allah, I am a raqub woman who does not bear; I had no child but him. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The raqub is she whose child remains. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No Muslim man or Muslim woman loses three children but He admits them to Paradise. Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: For you be my father, O Messenger of Allah, and two? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: And two."
"980","794 - With this chain: And a man of the Ansar would sit with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with him a five-year-old son of his; he died, and he grieved over him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Would it please you not to come to a gate of Paradise but find him calling you to it? He said: Yes. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Then it is as I tell you."
"981","Chapter: Death on Friday. 795 - Abu Yala said: Abu Mamar Ismail ibn Ibrahim narrated to us; Abdullah ibn Jafar narrated to us, from Waqid ibn Salamah, from Yazid ibn Aban al-Raqashi, from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever dies on Friday is protected from the torment of the grave."
"982","Chapter: Washing the dead. 796 - Abu Yala said: Abu al-Rabi narrated to us; al-Mutamir narrated to us, from Abu Abdullah al-Shami, from Abu Ghalib, from Abu Umamah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever washes a dead person and conceals him, Allah the Exalted purifies him of his sins; if he shrouds him, Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, clothes him of sundus."
"983","797 / 1 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik, from Ata, he said: I asked Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) about bathing from washing the dead? He said: You would have deemed your companion impure then."
"984","797 / 2 - And Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: Ablution suffices therefrom."
"985","798 - Abu Yala said: Abdan narrated to us; Abd al-Wahid narrated to us, from Yunus ibn Ubayd, he said: Al-Hasan saw no bathing upon one who washes the dead."
"986","799 - Al-Harith said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us; Maysarah ibn Abd Rabbihi narrated to us, from Abu Aishah al-Sadi, from Yazid ibn Umar, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah and Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them), they said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sermonized to us and mentioned the hadith; and in it: Whoever washes a dead person and fulfills the trust therein, for every hair of him is the freeing of a neck, and a hundred degrees are raised for him thereby. Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) said: How does one fulfill the trust therein, O Messenger of Allah? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He covers his nakedness and conceals his defect; if he does not conceal his defect nor cover his nakedness, Allah the Exalted exposes his nakedness before the creatures."
"987","800 / 1 - Ishaq ibn Rahawayh said: Abu Muawiyah informed us; Hisham narrated to us, from Fatimah, from Asma bint Abi Bakr: that she said: When I die, wash me, shroud me, embalm me, perfume me with incense, do not scatter embalming upon my shroud, and do not follow me with a censer."
"988","800 / 2 - Abd al-Razzaq informed us; Mamar narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, and he mentioned it with the wording: that she ordered her clothes to be perfumed over a censer, and not to be followed with a censer; and Asma bint Abi Bakr willed: Do not place embalming upon my bier."
"989","Chapter: The shroud. 801 - Ibn Abi Umar said: Waki narrated to us; Hisham ibn Sad narrated to us, from Hatim ibn Abi Nasr, from Ubadah ibn Nusayy, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best shroud is the hullah. Sent forth."
"990","802 / 1 - Abu Bakr said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us; Usamah ibn Zayd narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: On the day of Uhud, Hamzah (may Allah be pleased with him) was shrouded in a striped cloak; when his head was covered his feet showed, and when his feet were covered his head showed."
"991","802 / 2 - Abu Yala said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us with this."
"992","803 - Sulayman al-Shadhakuni narrated to us; Yahya ibn Abi al-Haytham narrated to us; Uthman ibn Ata narrated to us, from his father, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), from al-Fadl (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was shrouded in two white Sahuli garments."
"993","804 / 1 - Al-Harith said: Uthman ibn al-Haytham narrated to us; Abdullah ibn Ubayd al-Himyari narrated to us, from Udaysah bint Uhban ibn Sayfi, she said: I came when my father's death attended, and he said: Do not shroud me in a sewn shirt. They said: It must be; so I sent to the fuller, and my father had a shirt at fulling; it was brought and put on him, and he was taken; I closed my door and followed him, and returned to my house and found the shirt in the house; I sent to those who washed my father and said: Did you shroud him in his shirt? They said: Yes. I said: Is this it? They said: Yes. 804 / 2 - Ahmad brought it abridged, and its wording: From the daughter of Uhban: that her father ordered his family to shroud him and not dress him a shirt; she said: We woke with the shirt upon the rack."
"994","Chapter: Walking with the funeral and standing with it until burial. 805 - Ishaq said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad al-Muharibi informed us; Muttarih ibn Yazid al-Dimashqi narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Zahr, from Ali ibn Yazid, from al-Qasim, from Abu Umamah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: Abu Said al-Khudri said to Ali (may Allah be pleased with them both): Tell us, O Abu al-Hasan, about walking with the funeral; which of that is better? Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) said: By Allah, the merit of the walker behind it over the walker before it is like the merit of the obligatory over the voluntary. Abu Said (may Allah be pleased with him) said: By Allah, I have not sat since I witnessed a funeral witnessed by Abu Bakr and Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), and saw Abu Bakr and Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) walk before it. He said: May Allah forgive them; the best of this community after its Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) are Abu Bakr and Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both); then Allah knows best where the good is? If you saw them do that, they did so knowing that the merit of the walker behind it over walking before it is like the merit of the obligatory over the voluntary, as we know that before tomorrow is a night; but they liked people to spread out, and disliked that they crowd, and they knew they are followed. He said: O Abu al-Hasan, tell me about carrying the funeral; is it obligatory upon one who witnesses it? He (may Allah be pleased with him) said: No, but it is good; whoever wishes takes, and whoever wishes leaves; when you are with a funeral, put it before you and make it before your eyes, for it is an admonition, a reminder, a lesson; if it occurs to you to carry it, look at the rear left of the bier and make it upon your right shoulder, then the front left upon your right shoulder, then the rear right upon your left shoulder, then the front right upon your left shoulder. When you reach the graveyard, stand and do not sit, for you will see a grave matter; I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Your brother, your brother; he competed with you in this world and vied with you in it, then was straitened in the ease of the earth into palaces; he is entered into a grave beneath the hollow of a grave, turned upon his side; so stand and do not sit until you pour the soil upon him pouring. If the people do not leave you, and they will not leave you, and say: By Allah, this is nothing, then stand and do not sit until he is lowered into his pit, even if they fight you. I say: This is a very weak chain."
"995","806 - Al-Nadr ibn Shumayl informed us; Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Yala ibn Ata narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Yasar, he said: Amr ibn Hurayth visited Hasan, and with him was Ali (may Allah be pleased with them)... the hadith; Amr said to him: What do you say about walking before the funeral? He said: The merit of the walker behind it over the walker before it is like the merit of the obligatory over the voluntary. He said: I saw Abu Bakr and Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) walk before it. He said: They disliked to constrain the people. Ahmad narrated the story of the visit only, without what is at its end."
"996","807 / 1 - Abu Yala said: Ishaq ibn Abi Israil narrated to us; Ibn Wahb narrated to us; Ibn Lahiah informed me, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from al-Walid ibn Qays, he said: Abu Said al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) informed him that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever's fasting coincides with Friday, visits a sick person, attends a funeral, and frees a slave, Paradise becomes obligatory for him. 807 / 2 - Haywah followed him, from Ibn Abi Amr al-Khawlani, from al-Walid ibn Qays; Ibn Hibban brought it."
"997","808 - Abd ibn Humayd said: Abd al-Majid ibn Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Rawwad narrated to us, from Marwan ibn Salim, from Abd al-Malik, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The first with which the believing servant is rewarded after his death is that all who followed his funeral are forgiven."
"998","809 - Abd also said: Malik ibn Ismail narrated to us; Mindal ibn Ali narrated to us; Abdullah ibn Marwan narrated to us, from Nimah, from his father, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever prays over a funeral, it is as if he fasted a day in the path of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, and the day equals seven hundred days; and whoever witnesses the funeral of a Muslim, it is as if he fasted a day in the path of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, and the day equals seven hundred days."
"999","810 - Abu Yala said: Sahl ibn Zanjalah narrated to us; Abd al-Munim al-Basri narrated to us, from Abu Mawdud, from Humayd al-Kharrat, from Zayd ibn Thabit (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever prays over a funeral has a qirat; and whoever walks with it until it is buried has two qirats."
"1000","811 / 1 - Al-Fadl ibn al-Sabbah narrated to us; Abu Ubaydah narrated to us, from Muhtasib, from Yazid al-Raqashi, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him). He said:"
"1001","811 / 2 - And Umar ibn Shabbah narrated to us; Abu Bakr ibn Marwan al-Usaydi narrated to us, from Abd al-Warith, from Shuayb ibn al-Habhab, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), raised: No Muslim attends the funeral of a Muslim but he has a qirat of reward; if he sits until they pray over it, he has two qirats of reward, each qirat like Uhud."
"1002","812 - Al-Harith said: Hafs ibn Hamzah narrated to us; Sawwar ibn Musab narrated to us, from Umarah al-Hamdani, from Thawban (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever follows a funeral and takes the four sides of the bier, forty sins are forgiven him, all of them major. Weak."
"1003","813 - Abu Yala said: Sad ibn Ibrahim narrated to us; my father narrated to us, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: I saw Sad ibn Abi Waqqas (may Allah be pleased with him) in the funeral of Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf (may Allah be pleased with him), standing between the two front posts, placing the bier upon his shoulder."
"1004","814 - Umayyah ibn Bistam narrated to us; Mutamir narrated to us; al-Layth narrated to us, from Ibrahim, from a man, from Zayd ibn Arqam (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Allah the Exalted loves silence at three: at the recitation of the Quran, at battle, and at the funeral."
"1005","815 - Musaddad said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar; Ubayd, freedman of al-Saib, narrated to me that he saw Ibn Umar and Ubayd ibn Umayr walk before the funeral at upper Mecca, going ahead and sitting; when it passed them, they stood."
"1006","816 - Al-Tayalisi said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, from Said, from Abd al-Rahman, freedman of Abu Hurayrah, he said: Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) willed: When I die, do not pitch a tent over me, do not follow me with fire, and hasten with me."
"1007","817 - Abu Yala said: Abu al-Ashath narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Humran narrated to us, from al-Harith ibn Ziyad, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a funeral, and he saw women, and said: Do you carry him? They said: No. He said: Do you lower him? They said: No. He said: Return, laden with sin, without reward."
"1008","818 - Al-Harith said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us; Maysarah ibn Abd Rabbihi narrated to us, from Abu Aishah al-Sadi, from Yazid ibn Umar, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah and Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them), they said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sermonized to us and said: Whoever follows a funeral, for every step he takes until he returns is a hundred thousand good deeds, the erasing of a hundred thousand sins, and the raising of a hundred thousand degrees; if he prays over it, seventy thousand angels are tasked with him seeking forgiveness for him until he returns; and if he witnesses its burial, they seek forgiveness for him until he is raised from his grave. This hadith is fabricated."
"1009","Chapter: Standing for the funeral. 819 - Abd said: Yazid ibn Harun informed us; Ibn Awn informed us, from Nafi, he said: Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), when he saw a funeral, would stand until it passed him. And Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), when he went out in a funeral, would turn his back to the graves. Its chain is sound, and it is halted."
"1010","Chapter: The imam preceding in prayer over the funeral. 820 - Abu Bakr said: Bakr narrated to us; Isa narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: None prays over your funerals but me while I am among you."
"1011","Chapter: What is forbidden to follow the funeral. 821 - Abu Yala said: It was recited to Bishr, he is Ibn al-Walid: Abu Yusuf informed you, from Abdullah ibn Muharrar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): that he forbade a voice or fire to follow the dead."
"1012","Chapter: Burial. 822 - Abu Yala said: Abu Kurayb narrated to us; Waki narrated to us, from Shubah, from Abu Yunus al-Bahili, he said: I heard a man in Mecca whose origin was Roman narrate, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: A man would circumambulate the House saying: Awwah... awwah, in his supplication. He said: I went out one night, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was burying that man at night by a lamp."
"1013","823 - Ibn Abi Umar said: Sufyan narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from a man, from another of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) attended a dead one of ours to be buried, and said: Do not weigh down your companion. Sufyan said: Meaning that nothing be added over the soil of the pit; and sometimes he said in the hadith: Lighten for your companion. Sufyan said: Meaning, of the soil of the grave."
"1014","824 - Ahmad ibn Mani said: Hushaym narrated to us; Mujalid informed us, from al-Shabbi, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) willed me, he said: When you place me in my lahd, lay my cheek to the earth, so that nothing is between my skin and the earth."
"1015","825 - Ahmad said in al-Zuhd: Harun narrated to us; Damrah narrated to us, from Abd al-Hakam ibn Sulayman, he said: When the Jarif plague fell, Bushayr ibn Kab dug himself a grave, and recited the Quran in it until he sealed it; when he died (may Allah be pleased with him), he was buried in it."
"1016","Musaddad said: Umayyah, that is Ibn Khalid, narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Mutarrif, from Hakim ibn Qays ibn Asim who said: Qays ibn Asim (may Allah be pleased with him) advised his sons saying: Bury me where Bakr ibn Wa'il cannot see me, for I used to raid them in the pre-Islamic period."
"1017","Abu Ya'la said: Abdullah ibn Muti' narrated to us: Hushaym narrated to us, from Ziyad, from al-Hasan ibn Abi al-Hasan, from Qays ibn Asim who said: He gave advice, then he mentioned the hadith, and in it: Bury me in a place no one knows, for there were disputes between us and Bakr ibn Wa'il in the pre-Islamic period, and I fear they will bring it upon you in Islam and disgrace your religion."
"1018","Musaddad said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Abdullah ibn al-Asamm narrated to me, from his uncle Yazid ibn al-Asamm who said: When Maymunah (may Allah be pleased with her), who was his maternal aunt, died, I took my cloak and spread it in her grave, but Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) took it and threw it away."
"1019","Al-Tayalisi said: Qays and Sharik narrated to us, from Uthman ibn Umayr, from Zadham, from Jarir (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Make a lahd, and do not make a shaqq."
"1020","Al-Harith said: al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl narrated to us: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu al-Siddiq who said: When Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) placed the deceased in the grave, he would say: O Allah, keep the earth away from his sides and widen his pit for him."
"1021","Al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl narrated to us: Abd al-Warith narrated to us: Abu Jallas narrated to us: Uthman ibn al-Shammakh, who was the nephew of Samurah ibn Jundub (may Allah be pleased with him), narrated to me, saying: A son of Samurah who had begun to walk died, and he heard weeping. He said: What is this weeping? They said: For so-and-so. So he forbade them that, then called for a basin or a cup, and he was washed before him, then shrouded before him. Then he said to a slave of his: O so-and-so, go to his pit, and when you place him say: In the name of Allah and upon the Sunnah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and loosen the knots of his head and the knots of his feet, and say: O Allah, do not deprive us of his reward, and do not test us after him."
"1022","Chapter on burying the martyr where he is killed. 831 - Muhammad ibn Abi Umar said: Bishr narrated to us: Sa'id ibn al-Sa'ib al-Ta'ifi narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Mu'ayyah al-Suwa'i who said: Two men from the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were killed near the gate of Banu Salim, and that was mentioned to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he ordered that they be buried where they were killed. They were carried from where they were struck, and that coincided near the graveyard of Banu Hilal, so they were buried there."
"1023","Chapter on condolence. 832 - Ibn Abi Umar said: al-Muqri narrated to us: Sa'id ibn Abi Ayyub narrated to us: Umar ibn Abdullah ibn Shurahbil al-Absi narrated to me, from Talhah ibn Ubaydullah ibn Kurayz, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: No believer ever consoles a believer over a calamity except that he will be clothed on the Day of Resurrection with a robe in which he will rejoice."
"1024","Chapter on preparing food for the family of the deceased. 833 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from al-Hasan, from al-Ahnaf ibn Qays who said: I used to hear Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) say: No man from Quraysh enters through a door except that people enter with him, and I did not know the interpretation of his saying until Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) was stabbed. He ordered Suhayb (may Allah be pleased with him) to lead the people in prayer for three days, and ordered that food be prepared for the people. When they returned from the funeral, they came while tables had been laid, but the people held back from it due to the grief they were in. Then al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib (may Allah be pleased with him) came and said: O people, he has died ... the hadith. And it will come, if Allah Almighty wills, in full in the merits of Umar (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"1025","Ahmad said in al-Zuhd: Hashim ibn al-Qasim narrated to us: al-Ashja'i narrated to us, from Sufyan who said: Tawus said: The dead are tested in their graves for seven days, so they used to like that food be given on their behalf during those days."
"1026","Chapter on visiting graves and etiquette in that. 835 - al-Harith said: al-Hakam ibn Musa narrated to us: Ibn Abi al-Rijal narrated to us: Ibn Abi al-Zinad informed me, from his father, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban who said: The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I forbade you three things and I have now permitted them to you: I forbade you to make nabidh, so make nabidh, but every intoxicant is forbidden; and I forbade you to store the meat of sacrifices after three days, so eat and store; and I forbade you to visit graves, so visit them, but do not speak obscene words."
"1027","Abu Ya'la said: Amr ibn Husayn narrated to us: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us: Farqad al-Sabakhi narrated to us, from Jabir ibn Yazid, from Masruq, from Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: While we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at al-Abtah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood up cheerfully toward the graves, sat beside one grave, then sat with us sorrowful. We said: O Messenger of Allah, you left us before cheerful, and returned sorrowful? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I asked permission of my Lord to visit the grave of my mother, and He permitted me, or he said: He granted me leave, then I went to intercede for her but was prevented."
"1028","Musaddad said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us: Uthman ibn Hakim narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Sarjis and Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us that they heard Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) say: That I sit upon a coal and it burns what is beneath my flesh until it reaches me is more beloved to me than that I sit upon a grave. Uthman ibn Hakim said: And I saw Kharijah ibn Zayd in the graveyards, so I mentioned that to him, and he seated me upon a grave and said: That is only for one who innovates something upon it."
"1029","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Hammad ibn Khalid narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Abi Humayd narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ka'b, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever sits upon a grave defecating or urinating, it is as if he sat upon a coal."
"1030","Abu Ya'la said: al-Abbas ibn al-Walid narrated to us: Wuhayb narrated to us: Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir narrated to us, from al-Qasim ibn Mukhaymirah, from Abu Sa'id (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade sitting upon graves or praying upon them."
"1031","Chapter on burial in one grave. 840 - Ishaq said: Ahmad ibn Ayyub informed us, from Abu Hamzah al-Sukkari, from Jabir al-Ju'fi, from Thabit ibn Ubayd, from Jamilah bint Sa'd ibn al-Rabi' who said: My father and my uncle were killed on the day of Uhud, and were buried in one grave, and I found nothing of their inheritance; the allies took it. I say: Jabir is weak. And it has come that her uncle took what her father left, and that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prevented him after that at the request of her mother, and ordered him to give her half of it, in a story mentioned by al-Tabari and others in the commentary of Surat al-Nisa."
"1032","Chapter on dislike of sudden death. 841 / 1 - Abu Dawud: Durust, that is Ibn Ziyad, narrated to us, from Yazid, that is al-Raqashi, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: A man was with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he died, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was informed that he had died. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The one who was with us a moment ago? They said: Yes. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: As if it is a seizure in anger."
"1033","841 / 2 - Musaddad said: Durust ibn Ziyad narrated to us: Yazid narrated to us: Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to us with it."
"1034","841 / 3 - Abu Ya'la said: Ishaq ibn Abi Isra'il narrated to us: Durust ibn Ziyad narrated to us with it."
"1035","Chapter on the merit of one who dies upon his bed. 842 / 1 - al-Harith said: al-Muqri narrated to us: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad ibn An'um narrated to us, from Ya'qub al-Ma'afiri, from Abdullah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) said: Allah (Mighty and Majestic) is more protective of the blood of His believing servant than any of you of his precious wealth, until He takes him upon his bed."
"1036","842 / 2 - Abu Ya'la said: Abu Khaythamah narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Yazid al-Muqri narrated to us with it."
"1037","Chapter on permission to weep over the deceased. 843 - Ishaq: Muhammad ibn Bishr al-Abdi informed us: Muhammad ibn Amr narrated to us: al-Ash'ath ibn Ishaq narrated to me, from Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that day clasped his knees, then an angel entered and found no seat. He said: So I made room for him, while the mother of Sa'd, meaning Ibn Mu'adh, was weeping for him saying ... Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: All weeping women lie except the mother of Sa'd."
"1038","844 / 1 - Abu Bakr said: Ali ibn Hashim narrated to us: Ibn Abi Layla narrated to us, from Ata, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took the hand of Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf (may Allah be pleased with him) ... then he mentioned the hadith concerning Ibrahim, the son of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: This is only mercy, and whoever does not show mercy will not be shown mercy. O Ibrahim, were it not that it is a true matter, a truthful promise, a path we shall come to, and that our latter ones will join our former ones, we would grieve for you with a grief severer than this. And we are grieved by you: the eye weeps and the heart grieves, and we do not say what angers the Lord."
"1039","844 / 2 - Abd said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us: Ibn Abi Layla narrated to us with it. 844 / 3 - al-Tirmidhi brought its origin through Isa ibn Yunus, from Ibn Abi Layla, and said: And in the hadith there is more speech than this, thus pointing to what I mentioned here."
"1040","844 / 4 - Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated it, from Abu Awanah, from Ibn Abi Layla, up to his saying: This is only mercy, only. Abu al-Mughirah al-Nadr ibn Isma'il opposed them, and Isra'il followed him, so they both narrated it from Ibn Abi Layla, from Ata, from Jabir, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf (may Allah be pleased with him), making it from the Musnad of Ibn Awf. If it is preserved, it is as if Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) took it from him."
"1041","844 / 5 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: al-Nadr narrated to us: Ibn Abi Layla narrated to us, from Ata, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him), from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took me and admitted me into the date-palms ... then he mentioned the hadith in full, in separate pieces."
"1042","844 / 6 - Abu Ya'la said: Zuhayr narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us: Isra'il narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman, that is Ibn Abi Layla, then he mentioned it in full. Ibn Abi Layla is weak in memory, and the confusion in it is from him, and Allah knows best."
"1043","845 - Abd ibn Humayd said: Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us: my father narrated to us, from Salih ibn Kaysan who said: Abu Abd al-Rahman said: I heard Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then he mentioned the hadith concerning the permissibility of weeping over the deceased and concerning the ruling of wailing. And it will come, if Allah Almighty wills, in full in the Book of Asceticism."
"1044","846 - Abu Bakr said: Abu Khalid al-Ahmar narrated to us, from Mujalid, from al-Sha'bi, from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on the day Ibrahim died: Whatever grief is in the heart or eye is only mercy, and whatever is of voice or lamentation is from Satan."
"1045","Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr: Abu Salamah narrated to us that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) visited a man from Banu Mu'awiyah and found him dying, with his women around him weeping for him. Some men went to restrain the women, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Leave them, and when the soul is taken, then let me not hear the voice of a wailing woman. This is mursal with a good isnad."
"1046","848 / 1 - Ibn Abi Umar said: Bishr narrated to us: Hajib ibn Umar narrated to us, from Bakr ibn Abdullah al-Muzani that he complained. He said: I and al-Hakam came to him, and we discussed that the deceased is punished by the weeping of his family over him. So Bakr ibn Abdullah said: Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) said to a man from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Would a man go out striving in the path of Allah Almighty and be killed as a martyr in a region of the earth, then a foolish ignorant woman weeps for him, so he is punished by her weeping over him? The man said to Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him): The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) spoke the truth, and Abu Hurayrah was wrong."
"1047","848 / 2 - Abu Ya'la said: Zahmawayh narrated to us: Salih narrated to us: Hajib narrated to us, saying: I entered with al-Hakam ibn al-A'raj upon Bakr ibn Abdullah, and they discussed that the deceased is punished by the weeping of the living. So Bakr narrated to us, saying: A man from the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) narrated to us, meaning by that, as if Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) opposed him in that. So Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: That I go out, then he mentioned the like, but he said: He lied, instead of: He was wrong, and he repeated that."
"1048","Zuhayr narrated to us: Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Makhzumi narrated to us: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from Abd al-Hakim ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Farwah, from Ya'qub ibn Utbah, from Urwah, from A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: When Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr (may Allah be pleased with them both) died, he was wept over. So Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) went out to the men and said: I apologize to you concerning these women; they are fresh from the pre-Islamic period. I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The deceased is scorched with coals by the weeping of the living."
"1049","Chapter on expelling wailing women from houses, and rebuking wailing. 850 - Ishaq: Abd al-Razzaq informed us: Ma'mar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib who said: When Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) died, he was wept over. So Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The deceased is punished by the weeping of the living. But they refused except to weep. So Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said to Hisham ibn al-Walid: Stand and expel the women. A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: I adjure you. Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Enter, for I have permitted you. So he entered, and A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: Are you expelling me, O my son? He said: As for you, I have permitted you. So he kept expelling them woman by woman, while he (may Allah be pleased with him) was striking them with the stick. Then Umm Farwah went out, and he separated them, or he said: He separated the wailing women. I say: The marfu' portion of it is recorded with them. Ahmad narrated it from Abd al-Razzaq with this isnad alone without the rest of the story, and al-Bukhari alluded to the story in ta'liq."
"1050","851 - Abu Ya'la said: Abd al-A'la and Nasr ibn Ali narrated to us, separating them, both saying: Zakariyya ibn Yahya narrated to us, from Hushaym, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Suhayb, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Three things will remain in my nation until the Hour comes: wailing, seeking rain by stars, and boasting in lineages."
"1051","852 - al-Harith said: Ibrahim ibn Abi al-Layth narrated to us: Hajjaj al-A'war narrated to us, from Abu Bakr al-Hudhali who said: I said to al-Hasan: Did the women of the Muhajirun do what is done today? He said: No. Here are scratching of faces, tearing of collars, pulling of hair, and pipes of Satan: two ugly obscene voices: at this blessing, Allah Almighty mentioned the believers saying: In their wealth is a known right, for the beggar and the deprived, while you have placed in your wealth a known right for the songstress at this blessing, and for the wailing woman at calamity. The deceased dies with debt upon him and a trust with him, and bequeaths a bequest, then Satan comes to his family and says: By Allah, you will not execute his estate, nor return his trust, nor settle his debt, nor carry out his bequest, until you begin with my right. So they buy new clothes, then they are torn as work, and they bring them white, then they are dyed, then a canopy is erected for her in his house, and they bring a hired slave-woman who weeps with an eye of sorrow and seeks tears for their dirhams, and whoever calls her, she weeps for him for a fixed wage. She sings for their living in their houses and harms their dead in their graves. She prevents them their reward by what they give her of her wage. And what might the wailing woman say? She says: O people, I command you with what Allah forbade you: Allah Almighty commanded you with patience, and I forbid you to be patient, and Allah (Mighty and Majestic) forbade you impatience, and I command you to be impatient. Then it is said: Acknowledge her right, so drink is cooled for her, clothes are given, and she is carried upon mounts. To Allah we belong and to Him we return; I did not think ..."
"1052","853 / 1 - Musaddad said: Hammad narrated to us, from Mujalid, from al-Sha'bi, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He is not from us who shaves, or raises the voice, or tears."
"1053","853 / 2 - Abu Ya'la said: Ishaq narrated to us: Hammad narrated to us with it."
"1054","853 / 3 - Abu Khaythamah narrated to us: Yunus ibn Muhammad narrated to us: Hammad narrated to us with it."
"1055","854 - Abu Ya'la said: al-Qasim ibn Muhammad narrated to us: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Ubaydullah, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Upon wailing women are garments of tar."
"1056","Abu Ya'la said: Abu Ibrahim narrated to us: Isa narrated to us: Yahya narrated to us, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Any wailing woman who dies before repenting, Allah will clothe her with a garment of fire and make her stand before people on the Day of Resurrection."
"1057","He said: al-Rahawi narrated to us: Yunus ibn Muhammad narrated to us: Sadaqah narrated to us, from Layth, from Mujahid, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: I was in a funeral, and there was a woman therein, so he kept turning her back, but she paid no heed. So Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) said: O Mujahid, we seek reward, while she seeks sin."
"1058","Chapter on supplication in the funeral prayer. 857 / 1 - Ishaq: Isa ibn Yunus informed us: Uthman ibn Ata narrated to us, from his father, from Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) that when the deceased was brought and placed before him, he would face them saying: You have come as intercessors, so intercede for him, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: One hundred men are a nation, and a nation will not gather and sincerely supplicate for their deceased except that Allah grants them his sins and forgives them. This hadith is disconnected, for Ata al-Khurasani did not meet Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"1059","857 / 2 - Ibn Abi Umar said: al-Muqri narrated to us: Sa'id ibn Abi Ayyub narrated to us: Uthman ibn Ata narrated to me, then he mentioned the like."
"1060","858 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Abu Ahmad al-Zubayri narrated to us: Kathir ibn Zayd narrated to us, from al-Muttalib who said: Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) stood to pray over a funeral, so he uttered takbir then opened with the Mother of the Qur'an raising his voice with it, then sent blessings upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and uttered takbir and sincerely supplicated for the deceased, then uttered takbir and supplicated for the believing men and women, then turned to the people and said: O people, by Allah, I raised my voice with recitation only that you may know it is Sunnah. I say: The recitation of al-Fatihah and his saying it is Sunnah is in Sahih al-Bukhari."
"1061","859 - Abu Ya'la said: Zakariyya ibn Yahya al-Raqashi narrated to us: Asim ibn Hilal Abu al-Nadr narrated to us: Ayyub narrated to us, from Hisham, from his father, from A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say in prayer over the deceased: O Allah, forgive him, send blessings upon him, and bring him to the Hawd of Your Messenger. Asim is disputed over."
"1062","860 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Shu'bah: Zayd al-Ammi narrated to me, from Abu al-Siddiq al-Naji, from Abu Sa'id (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: We used to say in prayer over the deceased: O Allah, You are our Lord and his Lord; You created him and provided for him, gave him life and sufficed him. Forgive us and him, do not deprive us of his reward, and do not lead us astray after him."
"1063","861 - Yahya narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"1064","862 - Abu Ya'la said: Wahb ibn Baqiyyah narrated to us: Khalid informed us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that when he prayed over a funeral he would say: O Allah, Your servant and the son of Your servant, he used to testify that there is no god but You ... the hadith. Its isnad is sahih. Ibn Hibban brought it from Abu Ya'la, but it has a defect. al-Bayhaqi brought it from another way from Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, from Ubadah ibn al-Samit (may Allah be pleased with them both), as mawquf."
"1065","Chapter on the merit of funeral prayer, attending burial, throwing earth, and digging the grave. 863 - al-Harith: Dawud narrated to us, from Maysarah, from Abu A'ishah, from Yazid, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah and Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us ... then he mentioned the hadith, and in it: Whoever prays over a deceased, Jibril (upon him prayer and peace) with seventy thousand angels prays upon him, and what preceded of his sin is forgiven. If he remains until he is buried and throws earth upon him, he returns with for every step until he returns to his house a qirat of reward, and the qirat is like Uhud. Whoever digs a grave for a Muslim, Allah Almighty forbids him to the Fire and settles him a house in Paradise; if what is between San'a and Abyssinia were placed, it would contain them. This hadith is fabricated."
"1066","Chapter on takbir over the funeral. 864 - Abu Bakr said: Sa'id ibn Yahya Abu Sufyan al-Himyari narrated to us - Abu Bakr said: and he was a truthful man - from Sufyan ibn Husayn, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Umamah ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf, from his father (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to visit the poor of Madinah, then he mentioned the hadith, and in it: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) walked to her grave, prayed over her, and uttered four takbirs. And it will come, if Allah Almighty wills, in full in the chapter on prayer over the grave."
"1067","865 - Abu Ya'la said: Uqbah ibn Mukram narrated to us: Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ubaydullah al-Arzami narrated to us, from Ata, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed over his son Ibrahim and uttered four takbirs over him. Its isnad is feeble."
"1068","866 - al-Harith said: Hafs ibn Hamzah narrated to us: Furat ibn al-Sa'ib narrated to us: Maymun ibn Mihran informed us: Abdullah ibn Umar narrated to us, saying: The last of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) uttered takbir over funerals was four; and Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) uttered four takbirs over Fatimah (may Allah be pleased with her), and al-Hasan uttered four over Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), and al-Husayn uttered four over al-Hasan (may Allah be pleased with them both), and Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) uttered four over Yazid al-Mukaffaf, and Abdullah ibn Umar uttered four over his father (may Allah be pleased with them both), and the angels uttered four takbirs over Adam (upon them peace), and Ibn al-Hanafiyyah uttered four over Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) at Ta'if. This is a weak isnad."
"1069","867 - Abu Ya'la said: Ibrahim ibn Sa'id al-Jawhari narrated to us: Abd al-Samad ibn al-Nu'man narrated to us: Ali ibn al-Hazawwar narrated to us, from al-Qasim ibn Awf, from Husayn ibn Amir, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) uttered five takbirs over a funeral."
"1070","Chapter on rows over the funeral. 868 - Musaddad said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Husayn, from Musa ibn Talhah who said: I prayed with Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) over funerals of men and women, and he placed the men nearer to him and the women nearer to the qiblah, and uttered four takbirs."
"1071","869 - Ibn Abi Umar said: Bishr ibn al-Sari narrated to us: Hammad narrated to us, from Humayd, from Bakr al-Muzani, from a man from the Companions (may Allah be pleased with them) that he would place the men behind the women, and the women nearer to him."
"1072","870 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Muhammad ibn Yazid al-Wasiti narrated to us: al-Hasan ibn Umarah narrated to us, from Abu Sa'id Aqisa who said: I heard al-Hasan ibn Ali (may Allah be pleased with them both) say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: When the funeral is present and the ruler is present, the ruler is most entitled to pray over it."
"1073","871 - Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us: Husayn al-Mu'allim informed us, from Ibn Buraydah, from Imran ibn Husayn (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed over the mother of so-and-so during her postpartum, and stood at her middle. Its men are trustworthy, except that it is defective by the preserved report with this isnad from Ibn Buraydah, from Samurah, not from Imran, and the hadith of Samurah (may Allah be pleased with him) is in the Sahih."
"1074","Chapter on prayer over the child, and over the child of adultery. 872 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Ubaydullah: Nafi' informed me, saying: Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) prayed over a newborn in the house, then sent him and he was buried. I said to Nafi': Had he cried at birth? He said: I do not know. This is a sahih isnad."
"1075","873 - Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal narrated to us: Sa'id narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ka'b, from Maymun ibn Mihran that he witnessed Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) at a funeral, and people began whispering: He is a son of adultery. So-and-so said: It is said: He is the worst of the three. That reached Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), so he said: No, he is the best of the three."
"1076","874 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Salm ibn Salim narrated to us: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Jabir, from Amr ibn Yahya, from al-Nu'man ibn Bashir (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed over a woman who died in postpartum from adultery, and over her child."
"1077","Chapter on prayer over the grave. 875 / 1 - Musaddad said: Isma'il narrated to us: Ayyub informed us, from Humayd ibn Hilal who said: al-Bara ibn Ma'rur (may Allah be pleased with him) died before the arrival of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Madinah, and when he arrived he prayed over him. Its isnad is sahih, except that it is mursal."
"1078","875 / 2 - al-Harith narrated it connected, saying: Ya'qub ibn Muhammad narrated to us: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Qatadah, from his father, from his father (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed over the grave of al-Bara ibn Ma'rur (may Allah be pleased with him) and uttered four takbirs over him."
"1079","876 - Abu Bakr said: Dawud ibn Abdullah narrated to us: al-Darawardi narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Zayd ibn Qunfudh, from Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabi'ah, from his father (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a fresh grave and said: What is this grave? They said: The grave of so-and-so woman. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Why did you not inform me? They said: You were asleep and we disliked waking you. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not do so; call me for your funerals. Then he formed a row over her. Its isnad is hasan, and Ibn Majah brought it in brief."
"1080","877 - Abd said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated to us: Abu Bakr ibn Hafs informed us, from Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabi'ah who said: A woman used to pick up reeds and dirt from the mosque, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by her grave and prayed over her."
"1081","878 / 1 - Abu Bakr said: Sa'id ibn Yahya al-Himyari Abu Sufyan narrated to us - and he was a truthful man - from Sufyan ibn Husayn, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Umamah ibn Sahl, from his father (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to visit the poor of the people of Madinah and attend their funerals when they died. He said: A woman from the people of al-Awali died. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When she is ready, inform me. He said: They came to inform him of her and found him asleep while the night had passed; they disliked waking him and feared for him the darkness of night and the vermin of the earth, so they buried her. When morning came (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he asked about her, and they said that. So he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) walked to her grave, prayed over her, and uttered four takbirs."
"1082","878 / 2 - al-Harith said: Muhammad ibn Mus'ab narrated to us: al-Awza'i narrated to us, from al-Zuhri: Abu Umamah ibn Sahl narrated to me: Men from the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed me, then he mentioned the like, and in it: from the people of al-Awali; her illness had prolonged, and he used to ask about her from those present of her neighbors, and ordered them if anything happened to her to inform him of her. And in it: So they carried her and brought her to the place of funerals. They disliked rousing him from his sleep, so he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Why did you do so? Stand. So he stood and formed a row over her as is formed over funerals, and they formed rows behind him, then uttered four takbirs over her. Bishr ibn Bakr followed him, from al-Awza'i: al-Zuhri informed me. al-Bayhaqi brought it."
"1083","Chapter on prohibition of reviling the dead, and encouragement of good praise of them. 879 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from al-A'mash: Khaythamah ibn Abd al-Rahman informed me, saying: The likeness of one who reviles the deceased is like one overlooking destruction, and the likeness of one who sits upon the bed of the absent woman is like one whose backside is breathed upon on the Day of Resurrection."
"1084","880 - Abu Bakr said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, from Musa ibn Ubaydah, from Iyas ibn Salamah, from his father (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: A funeral of a man from the Ansar was passed by the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he was praised with good, so he said: It has become due. Then another funeral was passed by him and it was praised less than that, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It has become due. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, what has become due? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The angels are the witnesses of Allah in heaven, and you are the witnesses of Allah on earth. This is a weak isnad."
"1085","881 - Abu Ya'la said: Ahmad ibn Umar al-Waki'i narrated to us: Mu'ammal narrated to us: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Thabit, from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no Muslim who dies and four households of his nearest neighbors testify that they know only good from him, except that Allah Almighty says: I have accepted your knowledge, and forgiven him what you do not know."
"1086","Chapter on one who disliked prayer over the funeral in the mosque. 882 / 1 - al-Tayalisi said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from Salih, freedman of al-Taw'amah, who said: I met men from those who met the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him): when they came and found no place except to pray in the mosque, they returned and did not pray."
"1087","882 / 2 - Abd al-Razzaq said: al-Thawri and Ma'mar informed us, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, from Salih who said: I saw the funeral placed in the mosque, and I saw Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him): when he found no place except in the mosque, he left and did not pray over it."
"1088","Chapter on prayer over the absent. 883 - Abu Ya'la said: Yahya al-Himmani narrated to us: Hudayj narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Amir, from Sa'id ibn Zayd (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed over al-Najashi."
"1089","884 - Musaddad said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Your brother al-Najashi has died; stand and pray over him, or: stand and supplicate for him. This is mursal; its men are trustworthy."
"1090","Chapter on prayer over one who says: There is no god but Allah. 885 / 1 - Musaddad said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim: When he affirms Islam then dies and prayer has not been prayed over him, prayer is prayed over him."
"1091","885 / 2 - Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Mughirah the like."
"1092","886 / 1 - Abu Bakr said: Sharik narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Isa, from Abdullah ibn Jabr, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: A young Jewish boy used to serve the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he fell ill, so he visited him and said: Do you testify that there is no god but Allah and that I am the Messenger of Allah? He looked at his father, so he said to him: Say as Muhammad says. He said: So he accepted. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Pray over your companion."
"1093","886 / 2 - Abu Ya'la said: Abu Bakr narrated to us with it."
"1094","887 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: al-Hasan ibn Sawwar Abu al-Ala narrated to us: Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Mu'awiyah ibn Salih who said: Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Azdi narrated to him, saying: I heard Ibn A'idh say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out for the funeral of a man from the Ansar, and when he was placed, Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) said: O Messenger of Allah, do not pray over him, for he is a wicked man. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned and said: Has any of you seen him upon anything of the deeds of Islam? A man said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah, he guarded with us a night in the path of Allah Almighty. So he prayed over him, threw earth upon him, and said: Your companions think you are from the people of the Fire, while I testify you are from the people of Paradise. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Umar, you are not asked about the deeds of people, but you are asked about prayer."
"1095","888 - Abu Ya'la said: Harun ibn Ma'ruf narrated to us: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us: Bahir ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Khalid ibn Ma'dan, from Abu Atiyyah who said: A man died in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so some said: O Messenger of Allah, do not pray over him, then he mentioned the hadith in full."
"1096","Chapter on zakat of livestock. 889 - Ishaq: al-Nadr ibn Shumayl informed us: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, saying: This is a book the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote for Amr ibn Hazm concerning the obligations of camels and sheep: And concerning sheep, when they reach forty sheep, one sheep, until they reach one hundred twenty. When they exceed one hundred twenty, in them are two sheep, until they reach two hundred. When they exceed two hundred, in them are three sheep, until they reach three hundred. When they exceed three hundred and are more than that, count in every hundred sheep: one sheep. And concerning camels, in twenty-five is a bint makhad; if not found, then an ibn labun. When they reach thirty-six, in them is a bint labun. When they reach forty-six, in them is a hiqqah until sixty. Then in them is a jadha'ah until seventy-five. Then in them are two bint labun until ninety. When they increase, in them are two hiqqahs up to one hundred twenty. When they return, count back to the first obligation of camels: a sheep, until they reach one hundred twenty. When they are many, in every fifty is a hiqqah. Hammad said: Qays ibn Sa'd informed us of that, from Abu Bakr ibn Amr ibn Hazm."
"1097","890 - Abu Ya'la said: Abu al-Rabi' narrated to us: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, saying: I heard Ayyub, Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Sarrah, and Ubaydullah ibn Umar narrating, from Nafi', that he read the book of Umar (may Allah be pleased with him): There is nothing in less than five camels, the hadith. Like the book of Abu Bakr to Anas (may Allah be pleased with them both)."
"1098","Chapter comprehensive on zakat limits. 891 - Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah said: Ali ibn Hashim narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Abd al-Karim, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: There is nothing in less than five camels, nor in less than forty sheep anything, nor in less than thirty cattle anything, nor in less than twenty mithqals anything, nor in less than two hundred dirhams anything, nor in less than five awsuq anything. And the tenth is in dates, raisins, wheat, and barley. What is watered by flowing water, in it is the tenth; what is watered by bucket, in it is half the tenth."
"1099","892 - al-Harith said: Muhammad ibn Umar narrated to us: Usamah ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he obligated zakat in gold, silver, camels, cattle, sheep, wheat, barley, sult, and raisins."
"1100","893 - Musaddad said: Mu'tamir narrated to us, from his father: Khidash narrated to us, from Ikrimah ibn Khalid, from a man who narrated to him, from the zakat collector of Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with them both) whom he sent to Yemen, that he took from every ten cows a sheep, and claimed that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz (may Allah be pleased with him) ordered that from every thirty cows be taken a young bull, jadh', or he said jadha'ah, and from every forty a musinnah."
"1101","Chapter: No zakat in wealth until a year passes over it. 894 - Ishaq: Abu Khalid informed us: Isma'il ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, from Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with them) that he gave Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) a gift that was due to him from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: And I add to you that there is no zakat in it until a year passes over it. Isma'il is al-Makki, in him is weakness, and the gift is mentioned in the Sahih without this context."
"1102","895 - Musaddad said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Uqbah, from Muhammad ibn Uqbah, that is his brother, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad who said: Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him), when he gave a man a gift, would say: Have you wealth? If he said: Yes, he said: Pay its zakat; and if he had no wealth he said: Do not pay zakat on it, meaning the gift wealth, until a year passes over it. I say: Its isnad is sahih, except that it is disconnected between al-Qasim and his grandfather al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"1103","Chapter on dropping zakat from horses and slaves. 896 - Musaddad said: Mu'tamir narrated to us, from his father: Azrah narrated to me, saying: The people of Sham said to Umar (may Allah be pleased with him): Our best wealth is horses and slaves, so Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) took for every horse ten, and for every head ten, for their provision, and he used to give them more than he took from them. Then these, meaning the officials of Banu Umayyah, took from the head ten and from the horse ten, and did not provide."
"1104","897 - al-Harith: Muhammad ibn Umar narrated to us, from Malik, from Abdullah who said: I asked Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib about mules, is there charity in them? So Sa'id said: There is no charity in any horses."
"1105","Chapter on dropping zakat from loaned wealth. 898 - Abu Ya'la said: Abu al-Rabi' al-Zahrani narrated to us: Sa'id ibn Zakariyya narrated to us, from Anbasah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Muhammad ibn Zadham, from Umm Sa'd al-Ansariyyah (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no zakat upon one who lends wealth. Its isnad is weak."
"1106","899 - Musaddad said: Sufyan narrated to us, saying: Ibn Shihab heard al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid say: I heard Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) say: This is the month of your zakat; whoever is in debt, let him settle, then leave what remains. Its isnad is sahih, and it is mawquf."
"1107","Chapter on taking the camel's rope in charity. 900 - Ishaq: Yahya ibn Adam informed us: Sharik narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Muhajir, from Ibrahim al-Nakha'i who said: Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: By Allah, if they withhold from me a rope from what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took from them, I would fight them over it. And he used to take with the camel a rope. Then he recited: And Muhammad is only a Messenger; Messengers have passed before him. I say: This is mursal, its isnad is hasan, and its origin was brought through a connected path; I only cited it for this addition that he used to take with the camel a rope, for it supports the narration of one who narrated in the well-known hadith: a rope, against one who said: a young she-goat."
"1108","901 - Musaddad said: Ibn Dawud narrated to us, from Ali ibn Salih, from Yahya who said: Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) consulted Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) about the apostates, so he said: Allah Almighty joined prayer and zakat, and I do not see they be separated. At that Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with them both) said what he said. Musaddad said: Al-iqal: the hundred camels of obligation."
"1109","Chapter on prohibition of taking the best wealth in zakat, and transgression in charity. 902 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Yahya, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad who said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) was passed by sheep from the charity sheep, in them a sheep with a huge udder, so he said: I do not think its owners gave it willingly. Do not take the best of the Muslims' wealth; do not tempt people; avoid food."
"1110","903 / 1 - Abu Bakr said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Mujalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, from al-Sunabih al-Ahmasi who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw a fine she-camel among the charity camels, so he said: May Allah kill him. So he said: O Messenger of Allah, I exchanged it for two camels from the inferior camels. He said: Then yes. 903 / 2 - Abu Ya'la narrated it from Abu Bakr."
"1111","904 - Abu Ya'la said: al-Qawariri narrated to us: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq: Salim Abu al-Nadr narrated to us, from a shaykh from Banu Tamim who said: He sat with me while I was in the mosque of Basrah in the time of al-Hajjaj ibn Yusuf al-Thaqafi, with a staff in his hand and a document he carried in his hand. He said: O Abdullah, do you see this book benefiting me with this companion of yours? I said: What is this book? He said: A book the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote for us. I said: How did he write for you? He said: I entered Madinah with my father, then he mentioned the hadith. Then my father said, meaning to Talhah: Take for us a book from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that there be no transgression upon us in our charities. He said: That is for every Muslim. We said: Even so. So he walked with us and said: O Messenger of Allah, these two chose that you write for them that there be no transgression upon them in their charities. He said: That is for every Muslim. He said: O Messenger of Allah, they chose that there be with them a book from you. So he wrote for us this book. Do you see it benefiting me with this companion of yours? For by Allah, transgression has been committed upon us in our charities. He said: I said: I do not think so, by Allah, then."
"1112","Chapter on commanding pleasure of the charity worker, and that the giver is absolved of what is upon him when he gives it to him, and sin is only upon one who abandons. 905 - Abu Bakr said: Khalid ibn Makhlad narrated to us: Thabit ibn Qays narrated to us, from Kharijah ibn Ishaq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Jabir, from his father (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Hated riders will come to you; when they come to you, welcome them, and leave them with what they seek. If they are just, it is for themselves; if they wrong, it is upon them. And please them, for the completion of your zakat is their pleasure, and let them supplicate for you."
"1113","906 - al-Harith said: Abu al-Nadr narrated to us: al-Layth narrated to us, from Khalid ibn Yazid, from Sa'id ibn Abi Hilal, from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: A man from Banu Tamim came and said: O Messenger of Allah, is Allah sufficient for me when I pay zakat to your messenger; have I become absolved of it to Allah Almighty and His Messenger? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you pay it to my messenger, you have become absolved of it; for you is its reward, and its sin is upon one who alters it."
"1114","907 - Musaddad said: Yahya, that is al-Qattan, narrated to us, from Ali ibn al-Mubarak, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir: Ba'jah ibn Abdullah narrated to me, from Zahir ibn Yarbu' who said: I said to Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him): Is the best of my wealth inviolable? He said: No. If they come forward, do not disobey them; if they turn away, do not greet them, lest he be disobedient staining other than wrongfully. Say less than the right; take the right and leave the false. If he takes, that is it; if he exceeds to other than it, be patient; Allah Almighty will gather for you on the Day of Resurrection in the Scale. Sahih mawquf."
"1115","Chapter on permissibility of advancing zakat. 908 - Abu Ya'la said: Humayd ibn Mas'adah narrated to us: Yusuf ibn Khalid narrated to us: al-Hasan ibn Umarah narrated to us, from al-Hakam ibn Utaybah and Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Musa ibn Talhah, from his father (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to take in advance the charity of al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib for two years. I say: Yusuf is destroyed, but he was followed; al-Bazzar said after bringing it from another way, from al-Hasan al-Bajali, from al-Hakam: This al-Hasan al-Bajali is Ibn Umarah; we do not know it narrated by other than him."
"1116","Chapter on permissibility of taking value in zakat. 909 - al-Harith: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us: al-Hajjaj ibn Artah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Tawus who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Mu'adh (may Allah be pleased with him) to Yemen, and he used to take clothes for the charity of wheat and barley."
"1117","Chapter on prohibition of charity upon Banu Hashim and their freedmen. 910 / 1 - Ishaq said: Qabisah ibn Uqbah informed us: Sufyan narrated to us, from Musa ibn Abi A'ishah, from Abdullah ibn Abi Razin, from his father, from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I said to al-Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him): Ask the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to employ you over charity. So he asked him, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: We do not employ you over the washings of people's sins. This is a hasan isnad."
"1118","910 / 2 - Abu Bakr said: Qabisah narrated to us with it."
"1119","911 - Ibn Abi Shaybah said: Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Abdullah ibn al-Harith, from Ibn Rabi'ah ibn al-Harith ibn Abd al-Muttalib (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: Banu Abd al-Muttalib walked to al-Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) and said: Speak to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for us that he place among us what he places among people of this collection and other than it. While they were thus conferring, Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) came; al-Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) called him and said: Your people and cousins gathered; would you speak to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for them that he grant them collection? Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Allah Almighty refused for you, O Banu Abd al-Muttalib, that He feed you the filth of people's hands. Rabi'ah ibn al-Harith said: Leave this; there is no good for you with him, then he mentioned the hadith. It is with Muslim, Abu Dawud, and others with its meaning; and it occurred with Muslim in a narration: Rabi'ah attacked him, and he did not explain that; and it was explained in this narration by his saying: And there is no good for you with this one."
"1120","912 - Musaddad said: al-Mu'tamir narrated to us: my father narrated to us, from Hanash, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: Nawfal ibn al-Harith sent his two sons to the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said to them: Go to your uncle; perhaps he will employ you over charities; perhaps you will gain something and marry. They met Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: Where are you going? They told him of their need, so he said to them: Return. They returned. When evening came, he, meaning their father, ordered them to go to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). When they were brought to the door, they asked permission. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her): Let down your curtain upon you; admit to me my two cousins. They told the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of their need, so the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: Nothing of charities is lawful for you, O household; it is the washing of hands. For you is a fifth, and in the fifth is what suffices you or enriches you."
"1121","913 - Abu Bakr said: al-Fadl ibn Dukayn narrated to us: Ma'ruf ibn Wasil narrated to us: Hafsah bint Talq, a woman from the tribe in the year ninety, narrated to me, from my grandfather Rushayd ibn Malik Abu Umayrah who said: I was sitting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) one day, when a man came with a tray upon it dates. He said: What is this? Charity or gift? The man said: Rather charity. He said: Then present it to the people, while al-Hasan (may Allah be pleased with him) was crawling before him. He took a date and put it in his mouth. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked, inserted his hand into the boy's mouth, pulled out the date and threw it, and said: We, the family of Muhammad, do not eat charity."
"1122","Al-Bawardi said: Muhammad ibn Ayyub narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us. And al-Tabarani said: Abu Zur'ah narrated to us: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, both saying: Muqarrin narrated to us with it."
"1123","And he said: Rushayd narrated to me. Ibn Mandah and Ibn al-Sakan both narrated it from al-Barudi with it. Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Tarikh from Abu Nu'aym with it."
"1124","914 - Abu Ya'la said: Zuhayr narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Asadi narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from al-Hakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Arqam ibn Abi al-Arqam over some charity; he passed by Abu Rafi' and took him along. He came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned that to him. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Abu Rafi', charity is forbidden upon Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad; and the freedman of a people is from them, or from themselves. Shu'bah opposed him, narrating it from al-Hakam, from Ibn Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi', from his father, from Abu Rafi' (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"1125","915 - Abu Bakr said: Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib who said: I came to Umm Kulthum, meaning the daughter of Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with them both). I entered upon her, and in the house was a bed woven with fiber, a pillow, and a hanging waterskin. I began looking, so she said: What are you looking at? We are in good from Allah; if we had only the charity of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) or Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), that would suffice us. He said: I said: Dirhams Salman (may Allah be pleased with him) bequeathed to a freedwoman of his called Ruqayyah? She said: I do not know her. I said to her: Take them. She said: I fear it is charity, and charity is not lawful for us; but go and give it in charity yourself. I said to her: Rather give it in charity yourself. She refused, then said: Last night a purse came from Iraq, and I returned it and refused to accept it."
"1126","Chapter on what zakat is taken from of grains. 916 - Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr narrated to us: Humayd ibn al-Aswad narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us, from Talhah ibn Yahya, from Abu Burdah ibn Abi Musa who said: Abu Musa and Mu'adh (may Allah be pleased with them both), when they were sent to Yemen to teach people their religion, did not take charity except from these four: wheat, barley, dates, and raisins. Abu Hudhayfah and al-Ashja'i followed him, from Sufyan, with al-Bayhaqi. He brought it from the way of Waki', from Talhah, saying: from Abu Burdah, from Abu Musa (may Allah be pleased with him) that he, then he mentioned it alone, and did not mention Mu'adh (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"1127","917 - Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Musayyibi narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Nafi' narrated to us, from Asim, meaning Ibn Umar, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What is rain-fed, or flood-watered, or moisture-fed, in every ten is one; and what is by watering, in every twenty is one."
"1128","Chapter on zakat of trade. 918 - Musaddad said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us from Yahya ibn Sa'id: Abdullah ibn Abi Salamah narrated to me, from Abu Amr ibn Himas, from his father, who used to sell hides and quivers, who said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said to me: Pay zakat of your wealth. I said: It is only hides and quivers. He said: Value it, then bring out its charity."
"1129","Chapter on zakat of jewelry. 919 - Ishaq said: Abdah ibn Sulayman informed us: Hisham ibn Urwah narrated to us, from Fatimah bint al-Mundhir, from Asma that she did not pay zakat on jewelry."
"1130","920 - Ishaq said: Jarir informed us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim that the wife of Ibn Mas'ud said: O Messenger of Allah, I have jewelry, and in my care are orphans; may I make the zakat of my jewelry for them? He said: Yes."
"1131","Chapter on the Imam's abstinence from taking charity. 921 / 1 - Abu Bakr said: Ibn Numayr narrated to us: Aban al-Bajali narrated to us: Amr, nephew of Ilba, narrated to me, from Ilba who said: Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Camels of charity passed by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he took a hair from a camel's back and said: I am no more entitled to this hair than a man from the Muslims."
"1132","921 / 2 - Abu Ya'la narrated it from Abu Bakr."
"1133","921 / 3 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us: Aban narrated to us with it."
"1134","921 / 4 - al-Harith said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us: Aban narrated to us with it."
"1135","Chapter on estimation in fruits. 922 - Musaddad said: Hammad narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Bushayr ibn Yasar, from Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah who said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) sent him for estimation of dates, saying: When you come upon a land, estimate it, and leave for them what they eat. Its isnad is sahih, and it is mawquf; and they brought with this isnad, from Sahl (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), as marfu'."
"1136","923 - al-Harith said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Aban narrated to us: Isra'il narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Abd al-A'la, from Ishaq ibn al-Hakam, from Muhammad ibn Rafi' ibn Khadij, from Rafi' ibn Khadij (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a man to a people, and he obliterated their date-palms against them. They came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: So-and-so came to us and obliterated our date-palms against us. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I sent him while in my view he is trustworthy; if you wish, take what he obliterated against you, and if you wish, we take it and return it upon you. They said: This is the truth, and by truth the heavens and earth stand."
"1137","Chapter on prohibition of night harvest fleeing from the poor. 924 / 1 - Musaddad: Yahya narrated to us: Ja'far ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from his father, from Ali ibn al-Husayn (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade night harvest and night cutting."
"1138","924 / 2 - Ahmad ibn Mani' and al-Harith, both: Yazid narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, from his grandfather with it."
"1139","Chapter on beginning with dependents in spending. 925 - Ishaq: Jarir informed us, from Ibrahim ibn Muslim al-Hajari, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: When Allah Almighty gives you good, begin with those you support, and give from surplus, and you are not blamed over sufficiency, and do not fail yourself."
"1140","926 - Musaddad said: al-Ala ibn Khalid, that is Abu Shaybah, narrated to us: Ata ibn Abi Rabah narrated to us, saying: I saw Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) circumambulating this House, calling: No charity except beyond the need of dependents."
"1141","Chapter on moderation in seeking provision. 927 - Ishaq said: Isa ibn Yunus informed us: Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid narrated to us, from Zubayd ibn al-Harith al-Yami, from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: There is nothing bringing you near Paradise and distancing you from Fire except that I commanded you with it; and there is nothing distancing you from Paradise and bringing you near Fire except that I forbade you it. And the Trustworthy Spirit breathed into my heart that no soul dies except that Allah Almighty has written its provision. So fear Allah and be moderate in seeking; and let not delay of provision carry you to seek it by disobedience, for what is with Allah Almighty is not attained except by His obedience. In it is a break."
"1142","928 - Abu Bakr said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, from Isra'il, from Mansur, from Salim, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A man from you comes to me and asks me, so I give him, then he departs carrying in his embrace only Fire. Sahih."
"1143","929 - al-Harith: Abd al-Rahim ibn Waqid narrated to us: Wahb ibn Wahb, that is Abu al-Bakhtari, narrated to us: Abbad ibn Kathir narrated to us, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah sends down provision according to need, and sends down patience according to affliction. Others followed him from Abbad, but Abbad is weak."
"1144","930 - Abu Ya'la: Ahmad ibn Isa narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to us: Usamah narrated to us, from Ubayd ibn Nistas, freedman of Kathir ibn al-Salt, that he narrated to him, from Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah Almighty gives His servant what He wrote for him of provision; so be moderate in seeking; take what is lawful and leave what is forbidden."
"1145","Chapter on prohibition of begging for one who does not need it. 931 / 1 - Musaddad said: Umayyah narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Mutarrif, from Hakim ibn Qays ibn Asim, from his father, that he advised his sons at his death: I advise you with fear of Allah, then he mentioned the hadith, and in it: And beware of begging, for it is the last earning of a man. Its isnad is good, and it is mawquf."
"1146","931 / 2 - Abu Ya'la said: Abdullah ibn Muti' narrated to us: Hushaym narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Abi Ziyad, from al-Hasan ibn Abi al-Hasan, from Qays ibn Asim, then he mentioned a hadith, and in it: And beware of begging, for it is the last earning of a person; and no one asks except that he offers his face's earning. "
"1147","932 / 1 - al-Humaydi said: Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Safwan al-Jumahi narrated to us: Hisham narrated to us, from his father, from A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Charity does not mix with wealth except that it destroys it."
"1148","932 / 2 - Ibn Abi Umar said: Muhammad ibn Uthman narrated to us with this."
"1149","932 / 3 - al-Bazzar said: Muhammad ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us: Uthman ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Jumahi narrated to us: Hisham narrated to us with it. I say: So it occurred, and I think it flipped and was altered. Ibn Adi said in the biography of Muhammad ibn Uthman that he was alone with it from Hisham. At the end of the hadith with al-Humaydi he said: Charity may have become obligatory upon you in your wealth which you bring out, but you do not bring it out, so the forbidden destroys the lawful. This is the explanation of the intended report, and it in what appears to me is the speech of al-Humaydi; it may be from other than him above him; and it may be that the intent is that a man takes zakat while rich without need, then adds it with his wealth except that it destroys it. This is from Imam Ahmad, and upon it I relied in bringing it in this chapter."
"1150","Chapter on one who said: In wealth is a right besides zakat. 933 - Abu Ya'la said: Abu Khaythamah narrated to us: al-Hasan ibn Musa narrated to us: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us: Abu Qabil narrated to us, saying: I heard Malik ibn Abdullah narrating, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him): That he came seeking permission upon Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him). Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Do not permit him. He sought permission, so Ka'b said: Permit him, may Allah set you right. He permitted him, with a staff in his hand. Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) said: O Ka'b, Abd al-Rahman, meaning Ibn Awf (may Allah be pleased with him), died and left wealth; what do you see? He said: He used to fulfill Allah's right in it, so no harm upon him. Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him) raised his staff and struck Ka'b, saying: You lied. I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: I do not love that this mountain be gold for me which I spend and it is accepted from me, leaving behind nothing of it. And I adjure you by Allah, O Uthman, did you hear? Three times. He said: Yes. He said: O Ka'b, be silent. He said: I find in the Torah which I narrated to you; Allah (Mighty and Majestic) said: Allah effaces what He wills, to the end of the verse. He said: Allah (Mighty and Majestic) effaced it. He said: I seek Allah Almighty's forgiveness. I say: The hadith of: I do not love that this mountain be gold is in the Sahih without this story, and without Uthman's (may Allah be pleased with him) saying that he heard it."
"1151","Chapter on rebuking begging. 934 / 1 - Ishaq: Abd al-Razzaq informed us: Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib and Urwah ibn al-Zubayr."
"1152","934 / 2 - Ma'mar said: Hisham narrated to us, from his father also, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave Hakim ibn Hizam less than he gave his companions. Hakim said: O Messenger of Allah, I did not think I would be shortened below anyone of people. So he increased him, then he asked increase, and he increased him until he was pleased. He said: O Messenger of Allah, which of your gifts is better? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The first, then he mentioned the hadith. It is in full in the two Sahihs; I only brought it for this added wording: Which of your gifts is better?"
"1153","935 - Abu Mu'awiyah informed us: Hisham narrated to us, from his father, from Hakim ibn Hizam (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: That one of you take his ropes, come to the mountain, bundle a bundle of firewood, place it upon his back, bring it to the market and sell it, and eat its price, is better for him than that he come to a man and ask him, whether he gives him or withholds, the hadith. Thus Ishaq narrated it from Abu Mu'awiyah; Ahmad ibn Abi al-Hawari followed him from Abu Mu'awiyah; and the isnad is sahih. But Waki' and more than one narrated it from Hisham, from his father, from al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam (may Allah be pleased with him); and from this way al-Bukhari brought it."
"1154","Chapter on warning against begging, and merit of giving. 936 - Abu Ya'la said: Abd al-A'la ibn Hammad, al-Abbas ibn al-Walid narrated to us, and I copied it from the book of Abd al-A'la, both saying: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Harmalah: A man from Judham narrated to me, from a man of them called Adi, between whom and his two wives was a dispute; he threw a stone at one of them and killed her. He rode to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was at Tabuk, asking him about the matter of the killed woman. He said: Pay her blood-money to her heir. Adi (may Allah be pleased with him) said: As if I look at the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon a red young she-camel of his, saying: O people, know that hands are three: the hand of Allah Almighty, it is the highest; the hand of the giver is middle; and the hand of the given is lowest. So refrain, even by bundles of firewood. Then he raised his hands and said: O Allah, have I conveyed?"
"1155","937 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Mansur, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Charity is not lawful for a rich one, nor for one of sound strength."
"1156","938 - Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us: Isra'il narrated to us, from Mansur, from Salim, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: One from you comes to me and asks me, so I give him, then he departs carrying in his embrace only Fire."
"1157","939 - Musaddad said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman narrated to us: al-Hajjaj narrated to us, from al-Hasan ibn Sa'd, from his father (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Charity is not lawful for one who owns fifty dirhams, or its equivalent in gold. Hadith of Hibban ibn Buh al-Suda'i will come, if Allah Almighty wills, in the signs of prophethood."
"1158","940 - al-Harith said: al-Hasan ibn Qutaybah narrated to us: Isra'il narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from a man, from Ibn Junadah, who had performed Hajj with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the Farewell Hajj, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever asks without poverty, it is as if he bites coals."
"1159","941 - Ishaq said: Abdah ibn Sulayman informed us: Hisham ibn Urwah narrated to us, from Fatimah bint al-Mundhir, from Asma bint Abi Bakr that she said to her daughters: Give charity and do not wait for surplus; for if you wait for surplus you will not find, and if you give you will not find its loss."
"1160","Chapter on the measure of the sa'. 942 - Ishaq said: al-Fadl ibn Musa informed us: al-Ju'ayd ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid who said: Their sa' that day was a mudd and a third of a mudd. This is sahih, and its origin is in al-Nasa'i."
"1161","Al-Harith said: Yahya ibn Ishaq narrated to us, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated, from Abu al-Aswad who said: Asma' (may Allah be pleased with her) used to say: We used to pay Sadaqat al-Fitr in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the mudd with which they traded among themselves."
"1162","Musaddad said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Mujahid, who said: The uqiyyah is forty, the nashsh is twenty, and the nawat is five."
"1163","Chapter of extending charity to all categories (26). 945 - Abu Ya'la said: al-Qawariri narrated to us, Kathir ibn Hisham narrated, Ja'far ibn Burqan narrated, Abu Sakinah al-Himsi narrated, from Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman who said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) came to al-Jabiyah, the Jabiyah of Damascus, and stood as a preacher, and he mentioned the hadith, until he said: Lo, when I leave this station of mine, let no one who has a right in charity remain except that he comes to me; and none of those present came to him except two men, so he ordered that they be given. Then a man stood and said: May Allah set right the Commander of the Believers, this rich man who looks after himself is no more entitled to charity than this self-respecting poor man. Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Woe to you, and how can we reach those?"
"1164","Chapter of carrying (people) on the camels of charity (27). 946 - Al-Harith said: Ashhal, that is Ibn Hatim, narrated to us, Ibn Awn narrated, from Muhammad who said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) asked a man about his camels, and he mentioned thinness and sores on the back, so Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I deem them bulky and fat. Then Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) passed by him while he was among his camels driving them and saying: Naqab. He said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: What is this? He said: The Commander of the Believers asked me about my camels, and I told him about them, and he claimed he deemed them bulky and fat, while they are as you see. He said: I am the Commander of the Believers, Umar; come to me at such-and-such place. So he came to him, and he ordered that they be taken and gave him camels of charity in their place."
"1165","Chapter of encouragement to pay zakat (28). 947 - Al-Harith said: Yazid, that is Ibn Harun, narrated to us, Abu Hilal al-Rasibi informed us, from Abdullah ibn Buraydah, from Ka'b, who said: No servant is honored before Allah the Exalted except that tribulation intensifies upon him, and no servant gives charity from his wealth and it decreases, nor withholds it and it increases his wealth, and no thief steals except that it is counted against his provision."
"1166","Chapter of Zakat al-Fitr (30). 948 - Ishaq ibn Rahawayh said: Waki' informed us, Hisham narrated, from Fatimah, from Asma', that she used to give Zakat al-Fitr on behalf of those of her family who died: the young and the old, the present and the absent."
"1167","Chapter of warning against hoarding wealth (31). 949 - Ishaq: al-Nadr ibn Shumayl informed us, Abd al-Jalil, that is Ibn Atiyyah, narrated, Abu Mijlaz narrated, who said: He stood and addressed - as if meaning the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - he said: Perished are the owners of purses, and I do not grieve over them, but over those for whom you pray. It was only that he came down and they feared to ask him, so they said: Whom do you think he meant? They said: We think they are people who will be after us, who will lock up this wealth and shed blood over it. I (Ibn Hajar) say: What is preserved is that this sermon is by Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"1168","950/1 - Abu Bakr said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, Bashir ibn al-Muhajir narrated, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No people ever withhold zakat except that Allah the Exalted withholds rain from them. This is a good (hasan) chain."
"1169","950/2 - Al-Ruyaniy said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, Ubaydullah ibn Musa informed us with it, and he did not say ever, and he said: rain is withheld, with dammah on the ha'."
"1170","950/3 - Abu Ya'la said: Zuhayr narrated to us, Ubaydullah narrated with it, and more complete than it."
"1171","951/1 - Al-Bazzar said: Bishr ibn Mu'adh narrated to us. 951/2 - And Abu Ya'la and al-Hasan ibn Sufyan both said: Umayyah ibn Bistam narrated to us, both said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, Sa'id narrated, from Qatadah, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from Ma'dan ibn Abi Talhah, from Thawban (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Whoever leaves behind a hoard, it will be likened for him to a bald serpent on the Day of Resurrection, having two spots, following him, and he says: Who are you? And it says: I am your hoard that you left behind. It will keep following him until it feeds him its hand and bites it, then follows the rest of his body. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no route, meaning to Thawban (may Allah be pleased with him), except this."
"1172","Chapter of urging charity and its merit (32). 952 - Ishaq: al-Nadr ibn Shumayl narrated to us, Abu Qurrah, that is al-Asadi, informed us, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: It was mentioned to me that deeds boast over one another, and charity says: I am the best of you. He said: And Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: There is no Muslim man who gives a pair of his wealth in charity except that the keepers of Paradise hasten toward him."
"1173","953 - Al-Nadr ibn Shumayl informed us, Hammad, that is Ibn Salamah, narrated, Ma'bad informed us, a certain man in the mosque of Damascus informed me, from Awf ibn Malik who said: Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him) sat with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the hadith like a hadith before it, in it: I said: O Messenger of Allah, what is charity? He said: Multiplied manifold, and with Allah is more. I said: O Messenger of Allah, which of them is best? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The effort of one with little, or secrecy to a poor person."
"1174","954 - Isa ibn Yunus informed us, Kathir ibn Abdullah ibn Amr ibn Awf narrated, from his father, from his grandfather (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The charity of a Muslim man lengthens life, prevents an evil death, and Allah removes arrogance and pride from him through it."
"1175","955 - Abu Ya'la said: Ibrahim ibn al-Hajjaj al-Nili narrated to us, Salih al-Murri informed us, from Yazid al-Raqashi, from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), I heard him say: Charity and maintaining ties of kinship, Allah the Exalted increases life thereby, repels an evil death thereby, and Allah repels thereby what is disliked and feared."
"1176","956 - Ahmad ibn Mani' : al-Hasan ibn Sawwar narrated to us, al-Layth narrated, from Shaybah al-Qurashi, from a man, from Abu al-Darda' (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever spends a pair of his wealth in the path of Allah the Exalted, the keepers of Paradise will call him from whichever of its gates he wishes."
"1177","957 - Abu Ya'la said: Abdullah ibn Muti' narrated to us, Hushaym narrated, from Ziyad, from al-Hasan ibn Abi al-Hasan, from Qays ibn Asim (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and when I drew near him I heard him say: This is the chief of the people of the tents. So I greeted, then sat, and said: O Messenger of Allah, what wealth is it upon which there is no liability for me from a guest who visits me, or dependents if they are many? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best wealth is forty camels, and more is sixty, and woe to the owners of hundreds, except one who gives from its milk on loan, and in its time of need, and lends its back, and lends its stallion, and slaughters its fat one, and feeds the contented and the beggar. He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, how noble and fine are these manners; the valley I am in is not free of my many camels? He said: How do you deal with lending a milch camel? He said: I said: I lend a hundred every year. He said: How do you deal with lending? He said: The camels go out in the morning and the people go out, so whoever takes a camel's head goes off with it. He said: How do you deal with lending a young camel? He said: I lend the first-calved she-camel with udder and the old she-camel turned away. He said: Is that dearer to you, or the wealth of your master? I said: Rather my wealth. He said: What is yours of your wealth is only what you ate and consumed, or wore and wore out, or gave and sent forth, and what remains belongs to your master. I said: To my master? He said: Yes. I said: By Allah, if I remain I shall surely leave its number small. Al-Hasan said: And he did so, may Allah have mercy on him."
"1178","958 - Sufyan ibn Waki' narrated to us, my father narrated, from Humayd, from Abdullah ibn al-Harith, who said: Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no morning except that a caller calls from heaven: O Allah, give every spender a replacement, and every withholder ruin; O seeker of good, come forward, and O seeker of evil, desist."
"1179","959/1 - Musaddad said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, Layth narrated, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said - I thought he raised it - he said: In the son of Adam there are three hundred and sixty joints, or bones, or articulations; upon each one of them every day is a charity. He said: A good word a man speaks is charity, helping a man his brother in something is charity, a drink of water you give to drink is charity, and removing harm from the road is charity."
"1180","959/2 - Abu Ya'la said: Abu Ma'mar al-Qati'i narrated to us, Abu al-Ahwas narrated, from Simak, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) similar to it."
"1181","959/3 - He said: And Muhammad ibn Bakkar narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Abi Thawr narrated, from Simak with it, but he said: Every joint of man comes in the morning with a prayer due, and every step one of you takes toward prayer is a prayer, and carrying for the weak is a prayer."
"1182","960 - Al-Harith said: Abu al-Nadr narrated to us, Layth narrated, from Khalid ibn Yazid, from Sa'id ibn Abi Hilal, from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: A man of Banu Tamim came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I am one of much wealth, with family and settled folk; tell me how I should spend and what I should do? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You pay zakat from your wealth, for it is a purification purifying you, and you maintain your relatives, and you recognize the right of the asker, the neighbor, and the poor, and give the near relative his due - the verse."
"1183","961 - He said: And Yahya ibn Abi Bukayr narrated to us, Hammad ibn Yahya narrated, from Yahya, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: O A'ishah, spend, and do not tie up lest it be tied up against you."
"1184","962 - Abu Ya'la said: Bundar narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Uthman al-Bakrawi narrated, from Isma'il al-Makki, from Abu Raja', from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Guard yourselves from the Fire even with half a date."
"1185","963 - Muhammad ibn Isma'il al-Wasawisi narrated to us, Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Sulayman ibn al-Ghasil, from Shurahbil ibn Sa'd, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, from Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with them) who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say upon the sticks of the pulpit: Guard yourselves from the Fire even with half a date, for it straightens crookedness, repels an evil death, and benefits the hungry as it does the full."
"1186","964 - Musaddad said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu al-Anbas, from al-Qasim, from A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: For me to give this ring of mine in charity to a poor person is dearer to me than a thousand dirhams I dedicate to the House."
"1187","965 - Abu Bakr said: Khalid ibn Makhlad narrated to us, from Musa ibn Ya'qub, my aunt Quraybah bint Abdullah ibn Wahb ibn Zam'ah informed me, from her mother Karimah bint al-Miqdad, from Duba'ah bint al-Zubayr, from al-Miqdad ibn al-Aswad (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Something I heard from you about which I am doubtful. He said: When one of you doubts a matter, let him ask me about it. He said: Your saying about your wives: I hope for them after me the truthful ones. He said: Whom do you mean by the truthful ones? He said: We said: Our children who perish young. He said: No, but the truthful ones are those who give charity."
"1188","967 - Abu Ya'la: Rawh ibn Hatim narrated to us, Hushaym narrated, from al-Kawthar ibn Hakim, from Makhul, who said: It reached me, from Hudhayfah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Lo, there will come upon you a biting time, the believer clinging to what is in his hand for fear of spending, while Allah the Exalted says: And whatever you spend of anything, He replaces it, and He is the best of providers."
"1189","Chapter of the best charity (33). 968 - Musaddad said: Abd al-Warith narrated to us, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, who said: Sa'd (may Allah be pleased with him) said: O Messenger of Allah, command me a charity. So he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Give water to drink, meaning water. Al-Hasan said: So he set up two watering places between which I used to run as a boy."
"1190","969 - Yahya narrated to us, from Yahya, from al-Qasim who said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr (may Allah be pleased with them both) died, and A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) gave charity on his behalf with slaves she owned."
"1191","970 - Abu Bakr said: Ahmad ibn Abdullah, that is Ibn Yunus, narrated to us, Umm al-Aswad narrated to me, from Munyah, from the hadith of Abu Barzah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had nine wives, and one day he said: The best of you is the longest-handed of you. So each one of them stood placing her hand on the wall, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I do not mean this, but I mean the most skillful-handed of you."
"1192","971 - Al-Humaydi said: Sufyan narrated to us, they informed me from al-Zuhri, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his mother Umm Kulthum bint Uqbah ibn Abi Mu'ayt (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The best charity is upon the hostile relative."
"1193","972 - Abu Bakr said: Affan narrated to us, Hammad narrated, from Uthman al-Batti, from Nu'aym ibn Abi Hind, from Hudhayfah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I was leaning the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) against my chest, and he said: Whoever gives charity seeking the Face of Allah the Exalted, and it seals his end, enters Paradise."
"1194","973 - Al-Harith: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us, Maysarah ibn Abd Rabbih narrated, from Abu A'ishah, from Yazid ibn Umar, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah and Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them) both said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us, and he mentioned the hadith, and in it: Whoever gives charity, Allah the Exalted gives him for the weight of every atom of it like Mount Uhud of the bliss of Paradise, and whoever walks with it to a poor person has the like of that, and if forty thousand people pass it on until it reaches the poor person, each one of them has the like of that reward in full, and what is with Allah is better and more lasting for those who fear and do good. This is a fabricated hadith."
"1195","974/1 - Musaddad: Khalid narrated to us, from al-Hajari, from Abu Iyad, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: There is no Muslim who spends a pair in the path of Allah the Exalted except that the angels, with sweet basil with them, hasten him at the gates of Paradise: O servant of Allah, O Muslim, this is good."
"1196","974/2 - Ibn Abi Umar said: Husayn al-Ju'fi narrated to us, Za'idah narrated, from Ibrahim, from Abu Iyad, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said - while Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) was sitting with him: There is no Muslim who spends an expenditure in the path of Allah the Exalted except that the angels come on the Day of Resurrection with sweet basil at the gates of Paradise: O servant of Allah, O Muslim, come forward. So Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: O Messenger of Allah, this man has no loss upon his wealth? So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I hope you are among them."
"1197","Chapter of charity reaching the dead (34). 974 - Ishaq said: Umar ibn Hafs al-Dimashqi informed us, my father narrated to me, from Khawlah bint Fahd, who was under Hamzah ibn Abd al-Muttalib, who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, we were upon what you knew, and we have married into you, and Allah has placed good for us in marrying you, and my mother has perished; will it benefit her if I give charity on her behalf? He said: If you gave charity on her behalf even with a trotter, it would benefit her. I (Ibn Hajar) say: It is disconnected between Hafs and Khawlah."
"1198","Chapter of urging kindness and helping and rescuing the distressed (35). 975 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Abd al-Quddus ibn Bakr ibn Khunays narrated to us, from Talhah ibn Amr, from Ata', from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Every kindness one of you does to a rich or poor person is charity."
"1199","976 - Al-Tayalisi said: Abu Utbah narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to his Companions: Which people are best? They said: O Messenger of Allah, a man who gives his wealth and himself. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: An excellent man this is, but it is not he; rather the best of people is a man who gives his utmost effort."
"1200","977/1 - Al-Harith said: Ubaydullah ibn A'ishah narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Atiyyah narrated, from Thabit, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: All creation are dependents of Allah the Exalted, so the most beloved of them to Allah - Mighty and Majestic - are the most beneficial of them to His dependents."
